  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-14",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-14",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "What is Machine Learning?",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What does it mean for a computer to learn?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore machine learning as an approach to AI in which a computer learns from data rather than being told what to do. Students consider how a model is trained on examples to make predictions, contrasting this approach with the logic-based AI they have seen in the previous unit. Students then train a machine learning model of their own to recognize the doodles they draw.",
        "student_overview": "**What does it mean for a computer to learn?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore machine learning as an approach to AI in which a computer learns from data rather than being told what to do. You'll consider how a model is trained on examples to make predictions and how this differs from the logic-based AI approach you saw in the previous unit. You'll then train a machine learning model of your own to recognize doodles."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "Handling Uncertainty",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I describe how likely something is to happen?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how probability can be used to describe events and their likelihood. Students consider a variety of events whose outcomes are uncertain and quantify their probabilities, exploring how those probabilities can be useful in decision-making. Students then write Python programs that use randomness to simulate uncertain events.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Pizza Party Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can I describe how likely something is to happen?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore how probability can be used to describe events and their likelihood. You'll then write Python programs that use randomness to simulate uncertain events."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-2",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-3",
      "name": "Describing Data",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What information can be found in data?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore what data can tell us, using music as the entry point. Students start by analyzing real adult music genre preferences, then collect and plot their own class data to compare the two distributions to develop familiarity with categorical data. From there, they shift to numerical data and investigate whether an artist's social media following predicts how many people stream their music on Spotify, learning to spot relationships and outliers in a scatter plot. Along the way, students build intuition for when categorical data is the right tool and when numerical data tells the better story.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Music and Data Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**What information can be found in data?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore what data can tell us. You'll look at and collect real data to develop familiarity with categorical and numerical data.",
        "unplugged": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-3",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-4",
      "name": "Prediction Models",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Why are predictions useful?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore prediction as a way of navigating uncertainty, taking on the role of a small business owner running an ice cream shop. Students consider the kinds of questions a business owner might want answered and discover that machine learning offers two broad approaches to making predictions: regression, which predicts numerical data, and classification, which predicts categorical data. Students then experiment with a regression model and a classification model of their own, using them to make predictions about the shop.",
        "preparation": "- Open **Level 1: Regression** and **Level 3: Classification** to preview these widgets for students.",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Why are predictions useful?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore prediction as a way of navigating uncertainty, taking on the role of a small business owner running an ice cream shop. You'll consider the kinds of questions a business owner might want answered and discover that machine learning offers two broad approaches to making predictions: regression, which predicts numerical data, and classification, which predicts categorical data. You'll then experiment with using a regression model and a classification model to make predictions about the shop."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-4",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-5",
      "name": "Building Regression Models",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I use data I already have to make predictions about the future?**\n\nIn this lesson, students formalize their intuitions about prediction from the prior lessons by constructing regression models of their own. Students plot data points and identify lines that do a good job of capturing the relationship between variables. Students then use what they’ve learned from data to make predictions about future inputs and write Python programs that use predictions from linear regression models.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Ride Share Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, rulers",
        "student_overview": "**How can I use data I already have to make predictions about the future?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll formalize intuitions about prediction by constructing regression models of your own. You'll plot data points and identify lines that do a good job of capturing the relationship between variables."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-5",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-6",
      "name": "Testing Regression Models",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do I know if one regression model is better than another?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how to test and compare regression models. Students begin by informally judging two regression models against a set of data points, then confront the question of how to make that judgment precise. They then invent their own ways to quantify a model's \"loss\"—the errors it makes—and discover why some approaches can be misleading. Finally, they translate their thinking into Python by writing a loss function that compares two linear regression models on data of their choice, using the results to decide which model makes better predictions.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Exploring Loss Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How do I know if one regression model is better than another?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn how to test and compare regression models. You'll begin by informally judging two regression models against a set of data points, then confront the question of how to make that judgment precise. You'll then invent your own ways to quantify a model's \"loss\"—the errors it makes—and discover why some approaches can be misleading. Finally, you'll translate your thinking into Python by writing a loss function that compares two linear regression models on data of your choice, using the results to decide which model makes better predictions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-6",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-7",
      "name": "Selecting Data",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How is AI affected by the data used to train it?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore the relationship between data and models. They begin by exploring how a model trained on weather data from one location makes bad predictions when used to make predictions somewhere else. Students are introduced to a heuristic they can use to evaluate whether a dataset is biased or representative for the contexts in which it is used. Finally, students write code to explore a dataset represented as a list and evaluate the dataset for representativeness.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Exploring Data Bias Activity Guide (one for every two students)",
        "student_overview": "**How is AI affected by the data used to train it?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore the relationship between data and models. You'll use a heuristic to evaluate whether a dataset is biased or representative. Then, you'll write code to explore a dataset represented as a list and evaluate the dataset for representativeness."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-7",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-8",
      "name": "Building Classifiers",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can a computer make a prediction about something it’s never seen before?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore classification in more depth. Working as postmasters who want to know whether a day's mail will arrive late or on time, students discover the nearest neighbors algorithm: to label something new, look at the most similar examples you've already seen. They first apply the idea by hand on a single feature, then in code with multiple features. Students learn to represent a day's features as a Python dictionary, and explore how the prediction shifts as they change both the values of those features and the number of neighbors the classifier considers.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Nearest Neighbors Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can a computer make a prediction about something it’s never seen before?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore classification in more depth. Working as postmasters who want to know whether a day's mail will arrive late or on time, students discover the nearest neighbors algorithm: to label something new, look at the most similar examples you've already seen. They first apply the idea by hand on a single feature, then in code with multiple features. Students learn to represent a day's features as a Python dictionary, and explore how the prediction shifts as they change both the values of those features and the number of neighbors the classifier considers."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-9",
      "name": "Testing Classifiers",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What makes one classifier better than another?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how to evaluate a classifier. Students consider three metrics—accuracy, recall, and precision—and compare two models that fail in different ways, discovering that the best classifier depends on the cost of each kind of mistake. Students then tune a classifier of their own, adjusting its confidence threshold to balance recall and precision in a context they choose.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Berry Picking Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What makes one classifier better than another?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore how to evaluate a classifier. You'll consider three metrics—accuracy, recall, and precision—and compare two models that fail in different ways. You'll then tune a classifier of your own, adjusting its confidence threshold to balance recall and precision in a context of your choice."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-10",
      "name": "Sensing the World",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What does the world look like to a computer?**\n\nIn this lesson, students are introduced to how computers sense the world. Students first consider how computers \"see.\" They explore how a grid of bits can represent an image, coming to understand that computers sense the world through numeric representations. Students then consider how computers \"hear.\" They take on the problem of helping a computer tell two instruments apart, learning that the way a computer senses the world is determined by the way humans choose to represent it. As students build their own simple classifier, they see how those choices shape how well a computer can distinguish one instrument from another.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Seeing Through Bits Activity Guide (one for every two students). Optionally print copies of each variation, so different groups reveal different images.",
        "student_overview": "**What does the world look like to a computer?**\n\nIn this lesson, you're learn how computers sense the world. You'll first consider how computers \"see,\" then consider how computers \"hear.\" You'll then take on the problem of helping a computer tell two instruments apart, learning that the way a computer senses the world is determined by the way humans choose to represent it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-10",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-11",
      "name": "Evaluating the Impact of Models",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How do I evaluate whether a machine is making the \"right\" decision?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore the pervasiveness of predictive machine learning technologies and practice evaluating these technologies to ensure they are fair and safe. As students compare the outputs of models used in relevant contexts, they develop their model evaluation skills. ",
        "preparation": "- Print out copies of the Evaluating Models Ethically Activity Guide (one for every two students)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How do I evaluate whether a machine is making the \"right\" decision?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore the pervasiveness of predictive machine learning technologies and practice evaluating these technologies to ensure they are fair and safe. As you compare the outputs of models used in relevant contexts, you'll develop your model evaluation skills. ",
        "unplugged": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-11",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-12",
      "name": "Building Models Ethically",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Who's responsible for what a model does?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore ethical considerations that arise while building ML models by building their own regression models to predict snack ratings. Through practice, they get a sense of interpretability and accountability as factors in the development process.",
        "student_overview": "**Who's responsible for what a model does?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore the ethical considerations that arise while building ML models by building your own regression models to predict snack ratings."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-12",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-13",
      "name": "Image Classifier Project",
      "absolute_position": 14,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this lesson, students build a system that classifies images into two categories of their choice. Students start by collecting data and identifying relevant features that might be useful for making predictions. They label their data with features, split the data into training and test sets, and represent the data in a Python program. They then implement a distance function to be used in a nearest-neighbor classifier and reflect on the model’s capabilities.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Image Classifier Project Planning Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "In this lesson, you'll build a system that classifies images into two categories of your choice. You'll start by collecting data and identifying relevant features that might be useful for making predictions. You'll then implement a distance function to be used in a nearest-neighbor classifier and reflect on the model’s capabilities."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-13",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit2"
      }
    },
