        "activity_section.key": "4e1997ea-81f1-4a5f-81ac-d3f32816ebfc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e8d63a02-a271-4da9-b8c2-fc23cb2a198b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab99895b-f617-406f-bc58-bcfe77455036",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to raise their hands to vote again.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "While the other prior doodles are readily recognizable as cats or dogs, this doodle is more ambiguous. Optionally try to sow doubt about students’ preferred choice to drum up suspense (e.g., “Are you sure?”, “What about the picture makes you think that?”)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab99895b-f617-406f-bc58-bcfe77455036",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e8d63a02-a271-4da9-b8c2-fc23cb2a198b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86eedafa-4b7f-44f0-87ba-728f3e4d44d1",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal that the doodle represents a cat.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Looking at these doodles, what rules—if any—could we use to distinguish between cats and dogs?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to attempt to identify rules that would decide whether a doodle is a cat or a dog—and guide them to the realization this is a harder task than it first seems. Answers may attempt to create rules that rely on traits (e.g., “The cats have whiskers,” or “The dogs have floppy ears”). If students take this path, aim to identify counterexamples (e.g., “The dog on the bottom looks like it has whiskers too,” or “Not all of the dogs have floppy ears—what about the one in the top right?”). Alternatively, prompt students to think about whether their rule is generalizable beyond the doodles shown here (e.g., “All of the cats here seem to have ears at the top of their head, but what if someone didn’t draw the ears?”). Spend a few minutes debating these rules, ending when it seems the point about the task being difficult has been made."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86eedafa-4b7f-44f0-87ba-728f3e4d44d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e8d63a02-a271-4da9-b8c2-fc23cb2a198b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6d885c3-9f24-4cfb-bcf4-9f57189df01a",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we learned about the limitations of logic-based AI, we learned that there are some problems for which it’s difficult to describe a set of rules that lead to a correct solution. This is one of those problems. But, today, we’ll take a peek at how to overcome it—and how a computer might be able to learn rules for us: even rules we might not be able to describe ourselves!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6d885c3-9f24-4cfb-bcf4-9f57189df01a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e8d63a02-a271-4da9-b8c2-fc23cb2a198b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42f6298b-2460-43ba-993d-30753422e473",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42f6298b-2460-43ba-993d-30753422e473",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e8d63a02-a271-4da9-b8c2-fc23cb2a198b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ca9fc09-9c5f-4b38-9df2-03801fe8d2eb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students, by example, to the key ideas behind machine learning.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *machine learning*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce an example machine learning model.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Introduction to Machine Learning"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ca9fc09-9c5f-4b38-9df2-03801fe8d2eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3902771c-b1d4-4170-a81e-c9acb7e632ad",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One of the central goals of machine learning is to create a model. Generally speaking, a model’s goal is to make accurate predictions based on the inputs it is given. ",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Different students will have different prior knowledge about “models” in other domains, such as science. Depending on what your students bring up, or what needs you recognize they have, you may have a discussion about the idea of a “model”. Know that in this lesson, the fact that it’s not clear exactly what’s going on inside the “model” and how it learns its own rules, is a purposeful mystery. We are introducing the machine learning approach here, but to understand the details of how models work, the students will need to learn more later in the unit and in the course."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3902771c-b1d4-4170-a81e-c9acb7e632ad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8921906-fc41-443c-a354-f695bb589880",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s think about a model that could solve our problem of identifying cats and dogs. Such a model would take a doodle like this as input…",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8921906-fc41-443c-a354-f695bb589880",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd07967f-619c-4d22-bfe4-a422f36d5424",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "And, as output, the model would predict that the doodle represents a dog.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd07967f-619c-4d22-bfe4-a422f36d5424",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d9c427c-bf76-40a6-b9d1-da5e7e73d5b6",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Provide another example of a model’s input and output."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d9c427c-bf76-40a6-b9d1-da5e7e73d5b6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "98d6253a-392d-446e-bdea-129acc7074f7",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If the model instead got this doodle as input…",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "98d6253a-392d-446e-bdea-129acc7074f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "202ab5f5-82dd-44e2-b4a3-25c046b059f5",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It would predict that the doodle represents a cat.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "202ab5f5-82dd-44e2-b4a3-25c046b059f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ad0dd28-8583-452e-b901-21924f818ae9",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *data*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *machine learning model*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ad0dd28-8583-452e-b901-21924f818ae9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7bcbc54-2c3c-4e91-84cd-41d889b80451",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We saw that a machine learning model makes predictions. But what does it mean to say that a machine learning model \"learns from data?\" Let’s take a look.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7bcbc54-2c3c-4e91-84cd-41d889b80451",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c94b983f-5fd2-4577-9060-83052b075640",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce an example training process."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c94b983f-5fd2-4577-9060-83052b075640",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "048db4ef-f2f4-44ee-9a64-333a2240486f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "At first, a machine learning model doesn’t know anything about our problem.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "048db4ef-f2f4-44ee-9a64-333a2240486f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a03e66f6-5299-4ae0-b474-953c0cf84c3b",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When given an input, the best it can do is make a random guess.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a03e66f6-5299-4ae0-b474-953c0cf84c3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5d4b296c-3469-47fc-af2c-c56169ed8be7",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce example training data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5d4b296c-3469-47fc-af2c-c56169ed8be7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "856a3968-a42b-4d6e-89b8-f135f09d16c5",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Making a machine learning model is different from writing a logic-based AI program. Rather than writing rules yourself, you start with data that could help a model learn the rules. This data will, of course, have something to do with the problem you want your model to solve: for our problem, we could start with lots of doodles of cats and dogs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "856a3968-a42b-4d6e-89b8-f135f09d16c5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "61359b2e-a855-4838-b02e-eb8cb7adff1a",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "More specifically, we’ll start with lots of labeled doodles of cats and dogs. Ultimately, our goal is to have many example solutions to our problem that a model could learn from.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "61359b2e-a855-4838-b02e-eb8cb7adff1a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13e5e716-1c5a-49c5-9fb6-f26411d72cc2",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This data is then used to train our machine learning model. Ideally, by the end of training, a model has learned rules from our data that are flexible enough to work when presented with new data. Notice again that, rather than telling the model specific rules about cats and dogs ourselves, the model seems to learn rules on its own from many examples.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13e5e716-1c5a-49c5-9fb6-f26411d72cc2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15b0e1ab-24e6-49a2-b694-6478eccdc6dc",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *training*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15b0e1ab-24e6-49a2-b694-6478eccdc6dc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8e6226d-63d8-452b-9468-f6f774bd49c0",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As we just saw, training is an important step before a model becomes capable of making predictions. Without training, a model will make incorrect predictions, if it can make any predictions at all.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8e6226d-63d8-452b-9468-f6f774bd49c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6f019c3-730b-4c9e-8cc3-adfd501c27d5",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *training data*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6f019c3-730b-4c9e-8cc3-adfd501c27d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff15a78d-8cee-4877-b267-e5db1500ef54",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember the many labeled doodles of cats and dogs? That was the training data in our example—the data that gave the model many example solutions to learn from.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff15a78d-8cee-4877-b267-e5db1500ef54",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44de93f3-b97e-4b48-9822-15de89febab8",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Summarize the machine learning process."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44de93f3-b97e-4b48-9822-15de89febab8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52ee918e-0d5e-48e7-8288-e6566634f2d8",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To summarize, then, the machine learning process typically looks like this. First, start by gathering data a model could use to learn something about the problem you’re trying to solve. Then, use the data to train a model. Finally, after training is complete, use the model to make predictions about new data. Now, let’s see this process in action.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52ee918e-0d5e-48e7-8288-e6566634f2d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c80ef30-9af6-404f-9259-35967b2e4f51",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Visit the Doodle Identifier and walk through the process of:\n- Creating training data\n- Training a model\n- Using the model to make predictions about new data",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "An efficient example with a high success rate is differentiating between circles and squares. Call one category “Circle,” and the other category “Square.” Then, quickly draw examples of circles and squares, train a model, and show students that the model can distinguish circles from squares."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c80ef30-9af6-404f-9259-35967b2e4f51",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec16e1ba-ab4f-42a8-8017-d780ebd7ac0c",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students explore the machine learning process by training the Doodle Identifier.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Doodle Identifier"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec16e1ba-ab4f-42a8-8017-d780ebd7ac0c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d0159a15-936e-4eb2-aafc-6bebee365ca0",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It’s time for you to explore the machine learning process by training a model to recognize the doodles you draw.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d0159a15-936e-4eb2-aafc-6bebee365ca0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb5940f8-8ea5-436a-bd58-7b719ec1ed72",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Doodle Identifier"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb5940f8-8ea5-436a-bd58-7b719ec1ed72",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f14c0dee-c34a-44c3-b6e6-fe3bfdafeb99",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Levels 1–2.\n- **Level 1:** Students train the Doodle Identifier to distinguish between doodles of their own design.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks them to evaluate their Doodle Identifier, and to distinguish between machine learning and logic-based AI\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students train the Doodle Identifier on their own drawings, keep an eye out for these moments where students may get stuck:\n- **The Doodle Identifier seems stuck or slow during training.** It can take some time, particularly on less computationally powerful machines, to train the Doodle Identifier. Typically, you can expect the Doodle Identifier to take 2–40 seconds to train. Assure students that the training process will indeed finish.\n- **The Doodle Identifier doesn’t recognize students’ drawings.** The Doodle Identifier isn’t guaranteed to recognize students’ drawings 100% of the time. In fact, the Doodle Identifier may inaccurately identify students’ drawings for any of the following reasons:\n  - *Students’ training data isn’t like the example they’ve asked the Doodle Identifier to predict.* Use this moment to help students see that a model is only as powerful as its training data—if a model hasn’t seen similar examples, it may make mistakes.\n  - *Students’ drawings are too complex or detailed.* The Doodle Identifier is a small neural network that may not be suited to recognizing complex or detailed drawings. Use this moment to help students understand that, even with good data, models can make mistakes.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 as an assessment opportunity to check whether students understand the difference between machine learning and logic-based AI. Strong answers will point out that these are two fundamentally different paradigms for building AI systems: one involves writing rules in advance, while the other involves selecting data from which a machine could infer rules."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f14c0dee-c34a-44c3-b6e6-fe3bfdafeb99",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a34da9b1-0bee-456b-a901-16b12fe7810c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "32238783-7e64-49f9-b8ab-590b8c1e4e3b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How do you think the Doodle Identifier learned to identify doodles on its own?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share their intuitions behind how the Doodle Identifier works. Aim to draw out deeper thinking from students—if they say they think the Doodle Identifier \"learned from examples,\" ask them if they might make a guess about how the Doodle Identifier learns from examples. It’s not expected students will have a fully developed understanding. Rather, the point of the discussion is to reflect on what students’ don’t yet know—and to tee up what they’ll learn throughout this unit."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "32238783-7e64-49f9-b8ab-590b8c1e4e3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e75b79c-3534-4b9a-b36f-f6e4608821ab"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d67edff6-6e87-4333-9771-585f18ac247e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Though we don’t yet know exactly how a model learns, we’ll start to explore this question in this unit—and throughout the rest of the course.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d67edff6-6e87-4333-9771-585f18ac247e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e75b79c-3534-4b9a-b36f-f6e4608821ab"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0dca124c-e72c-4055-9da9-81c333c9c426",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0dca124c-e72c-4055-9da9-81c333c9c426",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e75b79c-3534-4b9a-b36f-f6e4608821ab"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2160a8bb-6930-4c59-9a5c-064159f75157",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2160a8bb-6930-4c59-9a5c-064159f75157",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2e75b79c-3534-4b9a-b36f-f6e4608821ab"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09e8ccae-a96c-4027-872f-b2e752235fd1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read aloud the three events described on the slide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students in pairs or in small groups and ask them to discuss together how they’d organize the events in order based on how likely they are to happen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which is the most likely? Which is the least likely? How do you know?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should make arguments for what events are more or less likely. A strong argument will point out the number of possibilities and use that to compare two different events. For example, a student might say that there are more birthdays than there are songs in the playlist, so it would be less likely that two people share the same birthday. If students struggle to describe which is more likely, you can prompt them with questions like “How many different birthdays are possible?” or “If there were only two songs on the playlist, would that make it more or less likely that your favorite song plays first?” Help students see that as the number of equally likely possibilities increases, the chance that any one of them happens goes down.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the correct ordering. The most likely even is the favorite song playing (1 out of 100 chance), followed by sharing the same birthday (1 out of about 365 chance), followed by guessing the phone passcode (1 out of 9999 chance)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09e8ccae-a96c-4027-872f-b2e752235fd1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "be5c174e-f76a-4337-bd9a-f6c72c87702d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6565e594-6271-4aaa-b642-b7b54edd178f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Often, AI doesn’t always know the answer for sure, so it needs to make a prediction. When AI predicts the weather, or suggests a movie that you might like, or answers a question, it won’t always be accurate. So we need strategies that let AI measure how confident it is about events that are uncertain.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6565e594-6271-4aaa-b642-b7b54edd178f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "be5c174e-f76a-4337-bd9a-f6c72c87702d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b38e93e9-6936-4679-a740-79d5194a4012",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b38e93e9-6936-4679-a740-79d5194a4012",
        "lesson_activity.key": "be5c174e-f76a-4337-bd9a-f6c72c87702d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71dd4495-c325-4210-8330-868fdaa3ea09",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn to describe the likelihood of events using probabilities.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _probability_.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Calculating Probabilities"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71dd4495-c325-4210-8330-868fdaa3ea09",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7737c898-03db-47b2-9796-7f8ba3158519",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are lots of times when we’re not sure what’s going to happen. For example, when you roll dice, you don’t know ahead of time what number you’ll roll. But you can use probability to describe how likely each possibility is.\n\nProbability is a number that goes from 0 to 1. If something has probability 1, it means there’s a 100% chance that it will happen.\n\nFor example, if you roll a die, what’s the probability that you roll a number from 1 to 6? All of the possible options are from 1 to 6, so there’s a 100% chance that you’ll roll a number that’s in that range. It has a probability of 1.\n\nIf something has probability 0, it means there’s a 0% chance that it will happen.\n\nFor example, if you roll a die, what’s the probability that you roll the number 7? Dice only go up to 6, so it’s impossible to roll a 7 with just one die. This event has probability 0.\n\nIf something has a probability between 0 and 1, it might happen and it might not. The bigger the probability, the more likely it is that it will happen.\n\nFor example, if you roll a die, what’s the probability that you roll an even number? Half of all the possible rolls are even, so it has a 50% chance. The probability is 0.5.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7737c898-03db-47b2-9796-7f8ba3158519",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c0b800f-eef4-4dd5-92b2-089e9e0e514f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through how students can calculate probability. To calculate the probability of an event when there are multiple equally likely outcomes, take the number of outcomes for the event and divide by the total number of possible outcomes. For example, there are 3 possible even rolls out of 6 possible rolls. The probability of an even roll is therefore 3 ÷ 6 = 0.5."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c0b800f-eef4-4dd5-92b2-089e9e0e514f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff93d2b7-93f4-4454-884b-a0099953e91d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Probability can be useful any time we’re not certain what the right answer is. Imagine you’re trying to plan a pizza party. If you don’t know how exactly how much pizza everyone will want, you have to make a guess about how much pizza to get to feed everyone. Probability can help you make a good guess.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff93d2b7-93f4-4454-884b-a0099953e91d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c455f061-8faa-4584-b8f0-ca121ca47773",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Pizza Party activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the Pizza Party activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the activity guide, help them think through probability calculations. In the first step, the table already provides the list of 9 outcomes, and students are responsible for adding values in each row together to get the total number of slices for each outcome. Students then use those outcomes to calculate the probability that they will have enough pizza. Keep an eye out for students who make the common mistake of only counting rows where the total number of slices needed is exactly equal to the number of slices ordered. Help those students think through the idea that they’ll have enough slices for the party as long as the number of total slices needed isn’t bigger than the number of slices ordered, but it’s okay to have extra slices leftover."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c455f061-8faa-4584-b8f0-ca121ca47773",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f947b95-8106-40a8-b7ae-7c1be308b2c0",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "For a party with just two people, we could write out all the possibilities ourselves to calculate probability. But if we had a party with many more people, it would start to take a long time to calculate it all by hand. We can use Python to write programs to help us estimate probabilities.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f947b95-8106-40a8-b7ae-7c1be308b2c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8983453d-a7cd-4c90-bedb-6807f579e4de",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students write Python code to estimate probabilities. In the process, they learn about modules and randomness in Python programs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _module_.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Simulating Random Events"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8983453d-a7cd-4c90-bedb-6807f579e4de",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5754e607-116d-4440-982f-2898b38d49d3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If someone else writes a Python module that does something useful, we can import that module into our own programs to use those features in our code.\n\nSo far, the programs we’ve written in Python do the same thing every time we run them. But if we want to build a simulation that can handle events that are uncertain, it’s helpful to have Python code that can be random sometimes. Python has a built-in random module that we can use for this.\n\nTo use a Python module, we first need to import it into our code.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5754e607-116d-4440-982f-2898b38d49d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa779b9a-ede1-4437-92ca-4fe8b9b6e483",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the syntax for importing a module into a Python program."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa779b9a-ede1-4437-92ca-4fe8b9b6e483",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08768652-6130-4ef2-a449-2433d9dd72ae",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The `random` module includes a function called `randint` that we can call. `randint` picks a random number between two values. In this case, it picks a random number from 1 to 10. To call a function from a module, we use the module’s name, followed by a dot, followed by the name of the function.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08768652-6130-4ef2-a449-2433d9dd72ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "393d8468-b02d-4b4c-892c-db62abf8b079",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the syntax for calling `randint`. Point out that the function takes two arguments: a minimum and a maximum value.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "393d8468-b02d-4b4c-892c-db62abf8b079",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0130556e-8e27-42e1-aae0-40d536b33df4",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When the program runs, the program picks a random number and prints it out.\n\nBut if you run the program again, you might get a different number.\n\nWe can use code like this to simulate our pizza party. With Python’s `random` module, we can randomly generate how many slices of pizza each person might want.\n\nLet’s try writing some Python programs that use randomness to simulate a pizza party.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0130556e-8e27-42e1-aae0-40d536b33df4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "abbd926f-5245-4c12-88b7-ada606997734",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Simulating a Party"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "abbd926f-5245-4c12-88b7-ada606997734",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "818a1d34-57f1-4fe0-be55-5b0a5038d066",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-4.\n- Level 1: Students use a random number generator to pick a random number of pizza slices.\n- Level 2: Students use a random number generator to simulate a pizza party.\n- Level 3: Students run a pizza party simulation multiple times and collect data on the results.\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check Your Understanding about how probability and simulation are useful for decision-making.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, check on how they use randomness to build simulations. Common mistakes students might make include:\n- Not importing the random module into their program. Remind students that to use a function defined inside another module, they need to import that module first.\n- Not knowing how to check if a party was successful. Help students think through the logic here: a party is successful as long as the number of slices needed doesn’t exceed the number of slices ordered. The random-number generator is used to calculate the number of slices needed, so that value can be compared against the number of slices ordered (42).\n- Not knowing what code should be inside the loop or outside of the loop. Guide students to think about whether the logic should happen once for the whole program, once for every person, or once for every simulated party. Depending on the answer, that can help students decide whether belongs inside the loop.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 4 as an assessment opportunity to check whether students understand strategies for reasoning about uncertain events. Strong answers might point out that simulation can be used to predict how uncertain events might play out, or that probability can be used to measure how likely something is to succeed. Students might also point out that they can make decisions that increase the probability that they get a good outcome. If students struggle to think about this question, ask them how they might use the simulation to decide how much pizza to order.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "818a1d34-57f1-4fe0-be55-5b0a5038d066",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4a13d85-3d29-42e2-970e-1b389a516f62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31c07842-1562-4794-9a9a-75b689889479",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31c07842-1562-4794-9a9a-75b689889479",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c2b9ecb-25b0-4ab6-9dd9-54cd8bdf6f2f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1237cd33-4957-4da7-a4f2-075c81451f48",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we’re going to explore different types of data, how to make sense of it, and what to use it for. Data gives us information about the world, and we can interpret and analyse it to try and answer questions. The data we’re exploring today is about music. 80 adults were asked what their favorite music genre is, and the results are here on this graph.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1237cd33-4957-4da7-a4f2-075c81451f48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "86ff23b5-1a68-4016-8d01-072174417a11"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2341cdd-4bb8-451d-8e0d-b03d93077bfa",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about this graph?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should begin noticing details in the data and demonstrating some ability to interpret those details. Strong responses might identify the most or least popular genres or express that there is a high or low amount of variability. If students are really struggling, walk them through how to make sense of the chart. You could say something like “The height of the first bar represents the number of adults who say Hip Hop is their favorite. It looks like it was about 11 people”. Or you could prompt them with specific questions like “What genre was the most popular?” \"What music genres had similar popularities?\" etc.\nIf students are simply reading out the numbers from the graph, prompt them to try and interpret the data in some way (such as identifying the most popular genre).\nTake 2-3 responses and then move on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How do you think the music preferences of our class might differ from the data for adults?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This question aims to prime students about what additional data might be useful for. Strong responses make hypotheses (e.g. “There will be fewer people who like Rock” or “The most popular genre will be different”). End the discussion with: \"Hold onto these ideas, we’re going to collect and graph data about our class’s music preferences and then we might be able to test some of these hypotheses..”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2341cdd-4bb8-451d-8e0d-b03d93077bfa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "86ff23b5-1a68-4016-8d01-072174417a11"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c067f153-f749-4f45-a263-008132aac842",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn to represent categorical data and practice interpreting it by comparing the class’s music preferences with data on adults’ music preferences.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Music and Data Activity Guide.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Graphing Categorical Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c067f153-f749-4f45-a263-008132aac842",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "882cb819-7096-4fc5-8b48-bec4e9eb9514",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’re going to collect and graph data on our classmates’ music preferences. Before we start, I’ll explain what kind of data we’re working with, and walk you through how to graph data on a bar chart.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "882cb819-7096-4fc5-8b48-bec4e9eb9514",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9ef9cf5-2ce9-473a-a89b-eed9963a58ef",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _categorical data_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9ef9cf5-2ce9-473a-a89b-eed9963a58ef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7bfa0dad-b1d3-414c-9f79-4dce036b2866",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Data about the mode of transportation you use to get to school, the color of your hair, or your favorite food would all be considered categorical data because it’s information that is divided into different groups. Categorical data is often represented in bar charts, and we’re going to practice creating those charts using the data about the music preferences of the students in our class.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7bfa0dad-b1d3-414c-9f79-4dce036b2866",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2501b553-071c-4dc3-be25-a251bd771f64",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the instructions to explain how the activity will unfold."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2501b553-071c-4dc3-be25-a251bd771f64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c2ca67c-8574-4cff-a331-9cc56d14d55d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before we begin, I’m going to show you how to plot categorical data on a bar chart.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c2ca67c-8574-4cff-a331-9cc56d14d55d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "94e8d29f-8b23-474d-95cd-194f5ff222b0",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Narrate the slides to scaffold graphing categorical data.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Gesture or point along the horizontal axis to draw attention to the appropriate section of the graph.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "94e8d29f-8b23-474d-95cd-194f5ff222b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "061aead7-b241-42f4-903e-bf034311ecea",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Collect class music genre preferences.\n- Read each music genre on the activity guide aloud, in order.\n- For each one, ask: \"Raise your hand if this is your favorite genre from the list\".\n- Count the raised hands and announce the number.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have the students graph their data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students graph the data, look for students who are struggling and provide support as needed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What did you notice about the similarities and differences between music preferences for adults and teens, based on the data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Look for responses that interpret the data (e.g. “we like Pop more than adults do”) as opposed to just stating numbers without explaining what they mean. Encourage students to compare their results to the predictions students made during the warm-up. Highlight that we were able to use the data to better understand and even answer some of the questions we came up with earlier."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "061aead7-b241-42f4-903e-bf034311ecea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1f7029b2-50c9-4124-8e63-0d59d104c7e8",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn to represent numerical data and practice interpreting it by plotting and analysing popular artists’ social media following and their Spotify listener count.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Graphing Numerical Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1f7029b2-50c9-4124-8e63-0d59d104c7e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb57da66-3c41-4cd1-98a1-6d903d53519f",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Next we’re going to explore a different type of data: numerical data.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb57da66-3c41-4cd1-98a1-6d903d53519f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b3f40b0-d8f3-40c1-a5ed-e11b6ed792fe",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\u000b<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _numerical data_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b3f40b0-d8f3-40c1-a5ed-e11b6ed792fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1689f9e1-ac1b-4c8f-a42d-7c6d0bb805e4",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Numerical data is often plotted on a coordinate plane, and we’re going to practice graphing and interpreting it.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1689f9e1-ac1b-4c8f-a42d-7c6d0bb805e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ccb336a0-bc06-43fd-99c5-9e46ca07c78a",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the instructions to explain how the activity will unfold.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ccb336a0-bc06-43fd-99c5-9e46ca07c78a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c499277e-da46-4ba9-ae7d-c5e13eefe1e4",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "I will show you how to plot the first two data points, follow along and then you’ll plot the rest in your Activity Guide.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c499277e-da46-4ba9-ae7d-c5e13eefe1e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c183d346-c4f2-4458-809b-86b78f31d228",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Narrate the slides to scaffold graphing numerical data.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Depending on the students familiarity with graphing, you may need to walk them through how they find a value like 48 along the axis.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c183d346-c4f2-4458-809b-86b78f31d228",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa09603b-d95c-4148-a06d-d5b4fdc793cf",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Take a few minutes to finish plotting the data in your Activity Guide.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Give the students a few minutes to finish plotting the data before navigating to the next slide, which has the final graph.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa09603b-d95c-4148-a06d-d5b4fdc793cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bcd1c812-e046-4afa-9769-dbfe9a9eb5d1",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Is there a relationship between social media following and listener count?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify a positive relationship between social media following and listener count. At the highest level, a student may say that the values are “positively correlated”. At a slightly lower level, they may point out that an artist with a higher follower count has a higher listener count. If students are struggling to see any relationship, prompt them with the question: “Do artists with a higher follower count have more listeners?” As the discussion wraps, if it hasn’t already been discussed, highlight that this data represents a positive correlation: as values increase across the horizontal axis, they also increase across the vertical axis.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students aren’t yet expected to use lines of best fit to describe the relationship. If students are already familiar with the concept and bring it up, that’s okay, but the goal here is to teach them to see the relationship broadly, as opposed to precisely describing the relationship mathematically.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bcd1c812-e046-4afa-9769-dbfe9a9eb5d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dcc124c7-c643-4f18-a375-60c62f604be9",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _correlation_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dcc124c7-c643-4f18-a375-60c62f604be9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb052be0-d713-4d57-859d-b499418f39c2",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If we had seen the opposite relationship, where artists with a higher follower count had fewer listeners and artists with fewer followers had more listeners, that would be a negative correlation. Correlations help us describe relationships in numerical data, but are not useful for describing and understanding categorical data.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "When explaining the negative correlation, it may be helpful to gesture - or draw - to demonstrate what the relationship would have looked like on the graph.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb052be0-d713-4d57-859d-b499418f39c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0362682d-2371-4321-9605-d201e33cab9f",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _outlier_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0362682d-2371-4321-9605-d201e33cab9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b8941a4-4137-4031-98b4-692dd7a9d4a7",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "How would you interpret the outliers in this dataset?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b8941a4-4137-4031-98b4-692dd7a9d4a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "943ea009-6151-4b33-beac-c325e1217b1d",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Take some ideas from students as to what outliers in data might mean: including people with a high listener count and low follower count, or people with a low listener count but high follower count."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "943ea009-6151-4b33-beac-c325e1217b1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c8ef6b5a-27be-4370-a146-b4f813c69662"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "059ccfc8-5960-410a-ba0f-bd594615f07f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kinds of information can categorical data help you better understand? What kinds of information can numerical data help you better understand?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should consider real-world situations where each data type is most useful. Some students might mention that categorical data is helpful when grouping or comparing things by type—like favourite music genres, eye color, or sports teams—where the values are labels rather than measurements. Some students might point out that numerical data is more useful when you want to track change, measure amounts, or find averages—like temperature, height, or number of streams.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "059ccfc8-5960-410a-ba0f-bd594615f07f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8101733b-bd69-4bc1-936e-7f037faba5cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88ae367f-70dd-4d6d-b952-c7123a63d46e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s Career Journeys connection: small business owners."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88ae367f-70dd-4d6d-b952-c7123a63d46e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2ffb79b3-d3af-4ebc-8c33-8f2c10bbd33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09f88682-122a-4fff-aa11-603f541ea843",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you’ll get to think like a small business owner. More specifically, you’ll see what it takes to run an ice cream shop!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09f88682-122a-4fff-aa11-603f541ea843",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2ffb79b3-d3af-4ebc-8c33-8f2c10bbd33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a3a25ef-8052-4d5c-8ee6-7ed813bee6f2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the typical challenges and uncertainties faced by small business owners.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the “I Bought An Ice Cream Shop” video (0:00–2:34)",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The portion of the video that best illustrates the challenges faced by small business owners is the recommended clip: 0:00–2:34. Beyond 2:34, the video begins to explore financial details that may or may not be interesting to your students. The video uses financial terms such as “capital”—a financial resource, such as cash, used to expand a business—and “debt”—money borrowed and later owed to someone else. Consider pausing the video and providing definitions for these terms if you think it’s unlikely your students will be familiar with them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a3a25ef-8052-4d5c-8ee6-7ed813bee6f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2ffb79b3-d3af-4ebc-8c33-8f2c10bbd33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c47b6396-ecee-4531-8d23-5523868eaafd",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce Anita’s Parlor, the business students will make predictions about.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Describe a few of the uncertainties Anita’s Parlor might face.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Motivate business owners’ need for predictions to help them successfully run their business.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *prediction*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c47b6396-ecee-4531-8d23-5523868eaafd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2ffb79b3-d3af-4ebc-8c33-8f2c10bbd33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8778083c-a830-4cc8-98be-15aa330d8b6a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we’ll explore different methods of prediction and what they can do to help us address uncertainties—like those faced by small business owners.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8778083c-a830-4cc8-98be-15aa330d8b6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2ffb79b3-d3af-4ebc-8c33-8f2c10bbd33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b35fc39-fa02-4223-820c-866d2742d166",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b35fc39-fa02-4223-820c-866d2742d166",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2ffb79b3-d3af-4ebc-8c33-8f2c10bbd33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15088a27-7b14-4b23-9618-e2bac935fbc9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Prompt students to put themselves in the shoes of a small business owner, illustrating the kinds of uncertainties it’s helpful to make predictions about.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Imagine you’re in charge of Anita’s Parlor. Your goal is to make ice cream and sell it for a profit. What uncertainties would it be helpful to make predictions about?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to think like a small business owner, recognizing the many uncertainties it would be helpful to make informed predictions about. Consider starting by having students get into pairs or small groups to discuss. Strong responses will be questions that 1) address something genuinely uncertain about the business, and 2) could be answered with a prediction. For example, “How many customers will buy vanilla ice cream?”. Be aware of students proposing questions that could be answered with a business decision rather than a prediction: for instance, “How much will we charge for ice cream?”. Try reframing such questions so they could be answered by a prediction in the face of uncertainty: for example, “How many people will buy ice cream if we charge $9 per scoop?”. When students have generated six or so useful questions, continue on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Keep track of students’ questions on the board or another location visible to the whole classroom. You’ll revisit their questions in this lesson’s activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Transition students from their class-wide discussion into an introduction to methods of making predictions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *prediction model*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Transition students from their class-wide discussion into an introduction to methods of making predictions.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Navigating Uncertainty"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15088a27-7b14-4b23-9618-e2bac935fbc9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b48dfba0-b063-4c44-96b0-ff44bceaf7ee",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to regression through a hands-on exploration of Anita’s ice cream shop data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *regression*.",
        "duration": "8",
        "name": "Regression"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b48dfba0-b063-4c44-96b0-ff44bceaf7ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0334185d-b184-4c65-b8a2-2590cb9a5606",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember that numerical data is data where each value is a number.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0334185d-b184-4c65-b8a2-2590cb9a5606",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c03a2e1-7720-4d1b-a33c-aa4057c90efa",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’ve learned that regression is a method used to predict numerical data. Suppose Anita’s Parlor wants to answer this question: ‘Based on today’s temperature, how many customers should I expect?’ This question is an example of one that could be answered with regression: it asks us to predict a number.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c03a2e1-7720-4d1b-a33c-aa4057c90efa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a27f6fb3-7e95-41c9-ad4c-1c9a7c716d6a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly preview the regression widget students will explore on their own."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a27f6fb3-7e95-41c9-ad4c-1c9a7c716d6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "803228a2-e406-4c90-974c-724c212ce626",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It’s time for you to explore regression on your own!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "803228a2-e406-4c90-974c-724c212ce626",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "12087aa2-11b5-4c99-8d76-8bcaef507a17",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Regression"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "12087aa2-11b5-4c99-8d76-8bcaef507a17",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85c4425c-7d1b-4bfa-a59a-e7fc2cb5cd84",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Levels 1–2.\n- **Level 1:** Students tinker with a regression model, using it to predict how many customers will arrive at Anita’s Parlor given a certain temperature.\n- **Level 2:** Students reflect on their experience in Level 1 and evaluate the regression model in simple terms."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85c4425c-7d1b-4bfa-a59a-e7fc2cb5cd84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80e8c4c6-1165-434e-b58c-059bfeb3ace3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to classification through a hands-on exploration of Anita’s ice cream shop data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define classification.",
        "duration": "7",
        "name": "Classification"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80e8c4c6-1165-434e-b58c-059bfeb3ace3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab991c08-73a5-4665-b8d8-166ac6bf4200",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember that categorical data is data that represents a category. Classification is used when we’re trying to put some data into defined categories.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab991c08-73a5-4665-b8d8-166ac6bf4200",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca946e31-4137-4467-a945-fe24557dc939",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’ve learned that classification is a method used to predict categorical data. The simplest scenario for this involves just 2 classes, so that’s what we’ll start with in this lesson. Suppose Anita’s Parlor wants to answer this question: ‘Will this customer leave a positive review?’ This question is an example of one that could be answered with classification: it asks us to predict a category—’yes’ or ‘no.’",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca946e31-4137-4467-a945-fe24557dc939",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5b79663-53f9-473d-bdd5-dd7e84c81b6f",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Demonstrate the example of classification students will explore on their own. Aim to demonstrate how to use the widget while saving most of the exploration for students themselves.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider clarifying to students that classification can involve more than two categories. For simplicity, the examples they’ve seen in this lesson include only two categories—but classification tasks often involve two or more categories."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5b79663-53f9-473d-bdd5-dd7e84c81b6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60cf67c7-b0ff-4343-a7e0-e6ca9bf20753",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It’s time for you to explore classification on your own!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60cf67c7-b0ff-4343-a7e0-e6ca9bf20753",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef9de551-01b5-4bff-ba1f-90c9b5a102ce",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Classification"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef9de551-01b5-4bff-ba1f-90c9b5a102ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "914f85ee-3abb-4899-b9f0-11b695108c95",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Levels 3–5.\n- **Level 3:** Students tinker with a classification model, using it to predict whether a customer will leave a positive review.\n- **Level 4:** Students reflect on their experience in Level 3 and hypothesize about how the classification model made its predictions.\n- **Level 5:** Students label scenarios as examples of classification or regression.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 5 as an assessment opportunity to check whether students understand the difference between classification and regression."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "914f85ee-3abb-4899-b9f0-11b695108c95",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b82eca85-19f5-4b0a-beb4-63c96a4d3671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "909b567f-f0b7-4a0b-b81e-1b78e024e86d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Revisit the questions faced by Anita’s Parlor you brainstormed as a class. Invite students to use their new knowledge of classification and regression to identify which kind of prediction might help them answer each question. Once students have worked with a partner, invite them to share out their thoughts with the class.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider revisiting each question from the prior discussion one by one. For each, ask the class if they think it’s a question that could be answered with classification or regression. Use mistakes as opportunities to reinforce the definitions of classification and regression students have learned."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "909b567f-f0b7-4a0b-b81e-1b78e024e86d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "831a929f-d04f-490b-8d69-b559778be3d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7fd20c4-7f1c-4102-8555-ca58d5726028",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7fd20c4-7f1c-4102-8555-ca58d5726028",
        "lesson_activity.key": "831a929f-d04f-490b-8d69-b559778be3d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e53aa2b7-4fc0-46fb-871f-5cfa45e95b91",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students begin thinking about relationships between variables and how they can use data about those relationships to make predictions.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e53aa2b7-4fc0-46fb-871f-5cfa45e95b91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "824459d2-58ef-40d7-a51f-e2869e33a528"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3ec8033-7a33-422d-8488-58713ed6e803",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s practice making some predictions. Here’s some data from a ride sharing app like Uber or Lyft, where you pay a driver to take you somewhere. Based on the data, how much would you expect a 10-mile ride to cost?\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3ec8033-7a33-422d-8488-58713ed6e803",
        "lesson_activity.key": "824459d2-58ef-40d7-a51f-e2869e33a528"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1849cec-c9e8-400b-b986-afd3afb2cd8b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students talk to a neighbor and discuss the data, the patterns they notice, and their prediction for the cost of a 10-mile ride. Then, take some possible suggestions from students.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What process did you use to make your prediction?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students discuss the process they used for making predictions. Strong answers will identify patterns in the data: for example, students might notice that when a ride increases in distance by 2 miles, it increases in price by $4. Some students might calculate a rate per additional mile, estimating either $2 per added mile; other students might have calculated an overall average price per mile (ranging from $2.38 per mile to $2.75 per mile). If students struggle to identify a pattern, prompt them with questions that guide them towards pattern identification (“What happens to the price as the number of miles increase? By how much does the price change?”) and then encourage students to apply their pattern to make a prediction. If students point out that it’s not possible to know for sure what the exact price will be, acknowledge that this is true — ride fares depend on more than just distance — but help students recognize that predictions are estimates and not necessarily guarantees of reality."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1849cec-c9e8-400b-b986-afd3afb2cd8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "824459d2-58ef-40d7-a51f-e2869e33a528"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "623103c7-aab8-4984-843c-20ade382303f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A lot of problems in AI look like this: we take some information and use it to create a strategy for predicting numbers.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "623103c7-aab8-4984-843c-20ade382303f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "824459d2-58ef-40d7-a51f-e2869e33a528"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b362c7a0-7887-4632-a418-4e0bb9666c9d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s Career Journeys connection: machine learning engineers, who build machine learning models to make useful predictions in real-world applications.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b362c7a0-7887-4632-a418-4e0bb9666c9d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "824459d2-58ef-40d7-a51f-e2869e33a528"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2d754597-8c76-4762-a095-64186d0e3240",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you’ll act as machine learning engineers, finding patterns in data and writing code that makes useful predictions.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2d754597-8c76-4762-a095-64186d0e3240",
        "lesson_activity.key": "824459d2-58ef-40d7-a51f-e2869e33a528"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "61ced288-3cad-402b-bd8c-f5f26f41ef70",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _regression model_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "61ced288-3cad-402b-bd8c-f5f26f41ef70",
        "lesson_activity.key": "824459d2-58ef-40d7-a51f-e2869e33a528"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b6fa57f-dfe4-4144-a783-c5020febf7a2",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we’ll be building regression models. Machine learning engineers use regression models to predict all kinds of numbers: prices, sports scores, time, weather, and more.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b6fa57f-dfe4-4144-a783-c5020febf7a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "824459d2-58ef-40d7-a51f-e2869e33a528"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72611394-cfd2-4775-80bd-fc5a21c8366f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72611394-cfd2-4775-80bd-fc5a21c8366f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "824459d2-58ef-40d7-a51f-e2869e33a528"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c887bc39-d66d-4303-9bf8-ef8123cbe2cd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students create their first regression models in an unplugged activity where they draw regression lines to model the price of ride shares. They use their linear regression models to make predictions about other ride prices.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _linear regression_.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Draw Regression Lines"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c887bc39-d66d-4303-9bf8-ef8123cbe2cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f68ea57-4036-48cc-8810-0061c97bd67b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Linear regression is a popular strategy machine learning engineers use to build regression models. In linear regression, we try to draw a line that matches data as well as possible, and then we use the line to make predictions.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f68ea57-4036-48cc-8810-0061c97bd67b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92481bcb-2eb8-4275-b7d2-3a2de34cfa2f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the animations to explain how to make a prediction based on a prediction line.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92481bcb-2eb8-4275-b7d2-3a2de34cfa2f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c13f8ebd-0213-442c-a0a2-dec2644579e5",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Say we wanted to use a linear regression model to predict how much a 4-mile ride would cost.\n\nFirst, we can find the point on the line that matches the 4-mile input on the x-axis.\n\nThen, we can get our prediction by checking where on the y-axis that point is. Here, the line is predicting $9.\n\nNow, you’ll have a chance to try constructing some linear regression models to make predictions about data.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be confused about the difference between the data points used to train a linear regression model and the points predicted by the linear regression model. Help students recognize that the training data is used to help draw a line that is a good fit. Once the line is drawn, the line can be used to predict new data points.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c13f8ebd-0213-442c-a0a2-dec2644579e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "639dd4b9-cde4-4583-8095-b909afa9df00",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair of students a copy of the Ride Share activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to work through the activity guide in pairs. Students will look at two data sets of ride share prices. For each data set, they’ll construct a linear regression model and use what they learn from their model to make predictions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the activity guide, help them think through the process of building models and using them to make predictions.\n- Some students might have questions about the meaning of the equation in Step 3. Help these students by walking through each part of the equation: they can predict the price of a ride by considering both the base price (the cost for 0 miles) and the price for the total miles traveled (the cost per added mile multiplied by the number of miles).\n- Some students might have trouble with drawing the regression line and completing Step 2 for the second data set. The second data set is more challenging because, while the data points do follow a trend, they don’t fall precisely on one specific line. Encourage these students to draw the best line they can: it might not be perfectly precise, but students should still be able to draw a line that gets close to most of the data points. It’s also okay for students to estimate the values for the boxes in Step 2, since the values might not be clean dollar amounts.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "639dd4b9-cde4-4583-8095-b909afa9df00",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "626cc215-9090-4393-959b-525f9be8b94d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve just used data to build some simple machine learning models by hand. Computers can do this too, but since they can calculate much faster than humans, they can build machine learning models that use much more data to make even more accurate predictions.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "626cc215-9090-4393-959b-525f9be8b94d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15aedda5-4600-443e-9db1-5abdc76a0a52",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students write Python code to implement a linear regression model that predicts a numeric output based on a numeric input. They use that linear regression model in a Python program that provides users with predictions.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Implement a Regression Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15aedda5-4600-443e-9db1-5abdc76a0a52",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1225700f-5069-4414-8c8b-a1ac49b8533c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you’ve had a chance to build some linear regression models, let’s learn some vocabulary that will help us talk about what makes up these models.\n\nThe kind of linear regression model that you’ve just built has two parts. The first part is the y-intercept.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1225700f-5069-4414-8c8b-a1ac49b8533c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f266283e-3a33-40f5-acdc-3bf1ff0cb685",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _y-intercept_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f266283e-3a33-40f5-acdc-3bf1ff0cb685",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "795b1d05-30e9-4eeb-8b5a-dcdb0a63c1a2",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In our ride share model, the y-intercept represents the base price you have to pay for a ride no matter how many miles you go.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "795b1d05-30e9-4eeb-8b5a-dcdb0a63c1a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8019603-6edf-4017-9c5d-82b8cafbeaa1",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _slope_.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8019603-6edf-4017-9c5d-82b8cafbeaa1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "26337867-7a85-4947-b853-1a4db2de0832",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We can measure slope based on how far up or down the line goes when we increase the x value by 1. In our ride share model, the slope represents the cost per added mile: it’s how much more you have to pay to go one more mile.\n\nOnce we’ve constructed a linear regression model, we can use the slope and y-intercept we’ve learned to make predictions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "26337867-7a85-4947-b853-1a4db2de0832",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9063fde6-86da-4fdd-bb81-91480a670730",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the equation on the slide to show how to predict an output by using the slope and y-intercept. The slope and the input are multiplied together and added to the y-intercept. Point out that this is the mathematical way of doing what students could do visually by looking at a drawn line.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students might recognize this equation from math courses as the equation of a line. You can let these students know that it’s the same equation: and the “linear” in linear regression means that we’re making predictions based on equations for lines.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9063fde6-86da-4fdd-bb81-91480a670730",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59e05e95-0cc8-4549-a4f7-8bbd3d5c2bdf",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s try writing some Python programs that use these ideas to implement a regression model.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59e05e95-0cc8-4549-a4f7-8bbd3d5c2bdf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "498a5ca4-388d-4bd8-9f90-f8f019766d76",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Implementing a Prediction Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "498a5ca4-388d-4bd8-9f90-f8f019766d76",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e358c16-0408-48eb-9979-cbd7f78b4b2e",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-3.\n- Level 1: Students implement a prediction model by using the input, slope, and y-intercept.\n- Level 2: Students use the prediction model they’ve created to write a program that makes predictions.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks students to interpret and explain a linear regression model.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, check on their comfort with implementing regression models. Common challenges students might encounter include:\n- Some students might not know how to combine values arithmetically to produce a prediction. Help these students walk through the reasoning for the mathematics: for example, every ride has a base price no matter how far the car travels, and a ride also has a price per mile. That price per mile needs to be multiplied by the total number of miles traveled.\n- Some students might need to be reminded of mathematical operators: in Python, the operator for multiplication is * rather than just an x, for example.\n- Some students might try to print output inside the `predict_cost` function. Remind students that a “return value” is an output for the function, and the `predict_cost` function should produce a prediction as output by returning a number.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 as an assessment opportunity to assess whether students understand how the equation for a linear regression model affects how the model makes predictions. Look for answers that identify that a new book might cost $20 and that for every year older a book is, it might cost $3 less. If students struggle to explain the code, ask them to give the function some sample inputs (e.g. 0 years, 1 year, 2 years) and walk through the steps of calculating what the return value will be.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e358c16-0408-48eb-9979-cbd7f78b4b2e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "110f9196-450c-4873-8ffb-3b16f710303a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "29c4f182-3fb0-4a2c-b1fb-ab46b7936b9a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "29c4f182-3fb0-4a2c-b1fb-ab46b7936b9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ee58a3d4-1b88-4eb8-9920-469748eadc18"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "54c2f389-48f1-4e9c-8a9c-4ec0c9b14f41",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to the concept of loss by inviting them to take sides in a debate.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "54c2f389-48f1-4e9c-8a9c-4ec0c9b14f41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7d569fa-4ca7-4c8d-a66e-2c10d6807cf5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Dani and Sasha are debating whose regression model is better.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7d569fa-4ca7-4c8d-a66e-2c10d6807cf5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67bc74a9-b6d6-4207-83c6-6326abadbc71",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Visualize the test data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67bc74a9-b6d6-4207-83c6-6326abadbc71",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b2436291-5ac8-4bf5-9caa-c20d04291b6f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "First, they plot some test data. Each point is an actual outcome their model should predict.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b2436291-5ac8-4bf5-9caa-c20d04291b6f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec9c609b-7257-48ef-8bab-7b27e502472a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show Dani’s model."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec9c609b-7257-48ef-8bab-7b27e502472a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6b8a281-b1e0-4782-b1ad-09e892695a19",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It seems like Dani thinks that, because her model gets more predictions correct, hers is the better model.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6b8a281-b1e0-4782-b1ad-09e892695a19",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "66dc6a9f-d42b-4129-8168-c6dde69d1527",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show Sasha’s model."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "66dc6a9f-d42b-4129-8168-c6dde69d1527",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "29d7339c-6460-4dbd-b74a-a39ade78804c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Sasha doesn’t seem to care how many predictions her model gets exactly correct, so long as all of its predictions are as close as they can be.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "29d7339c-6460-4dbd-b74a-a39ade78804c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8a6e4dc-0859-4b40-b145-9edf95b1d335",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Whose model—Dani’s or Sasha’s—is better? Why do you think so?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider their own, pre-lesson intuitions behind how to compare regression models. Guide students toward the realization that Sasha’s line is, in fact, a better regression model. Strong rationales can vary: students may point out that Sasha’s line better “fits” the data or that Sasha’s line is “closer” to all the data points. If some students argue that Dani’s line is better, acknowledge that—in some circumstances—they could be correct: there may be scenarios where it’s important a model get a few predictions exactly right. However, in most scenarios, it’s better to use a model whose predictions align with an overall trend in the data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *loss*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8a6e4dc-0859-4b40-b145-9edf95b1d335",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab93af70-ed75-47e3-b85a-1c99d6d3da4d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What Dani and Sasha were arguing about was *loss*: how wrong were their models, and whose was less wrong? Turns out there’s rarely a perfect regression model: as you’ll see today, testing regression models is about finding the model with the smallest loss.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab93af70-ed75-47e3-b85a-1c99d6d3da4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "feb7f8ea-552b-4dd7-8674-5a9897370306",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "feb7f8ea-552b-4dd7-8674-5a9897370306",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d17dda5e-bbdb-49ab-be45-5aadc33c0ec4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4c55b784-6a0a-491e-854b-14295d07c81b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Prompt students to get hands-on with calculating loss and comparing two models formally.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Exploring Loss Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Exploring Loss Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students fill in the Loss column, notice the range of strategies they invent or try. Look especially for students who keep the sign of each difference (i.e., a \"signed loss\" approach): their totals cancel out, so the first model sums to 0. Make a mental note of these students so you can call on them in the following discussion. Also watch for students who are stuck, either because the open-ended directions leave them unsure where to start or because they feel unsure how to turn three rows into one Total Loss. If a student is stuck, ask: \"Pick one row—how far off was the prediction from what actually happened?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you decide to calculate each model’s loss? Why did you decide to do it that way?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Get students to put their loss-calculation thinking into words. Most may describe some version of: \"Find how far each prediction is from the actual value, then add up those distances.\" Listen for any student whose method gave the first model a loss of 0. This usually means they used \"signed loss\"—treating an overshoot and an undershoot as canceling each other out. If so, push back: the first model clearly isn't perfect, so a loss of 0 can't be right. Use that contradiction to motivate \"absolute error\", where we count only the distance between a prediction and the actual value.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Exploring Loss"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4c55b784-6a0a-491e-854b-14295d07c81b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "126449af-e13f-499e-a6b3-8bf0df4b1071",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we’ve explored ways to calculate loss, let’s introduce some vocabulary to describe what we’ve just learned.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "126449af-e13f-499e-a6b3-8bf0df4b1071",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7f1fbcf-6167-4a1d-8ea9-a484c5dec927",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *test data*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7f1fbcf-6167-4a1d-8ea9-a484c5dec927",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d7e0844-2e6b-4433-af73-4d30e408629d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Test data is the data we use to assess a model’s loss. It’s kept separate from training data, which is the data we use to train a model. It’s important to test a model on data it has never \"seen\" before—that way, we can get a better sense of how the model performs in the real world.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As you define test data and the following vocabulary terms, consider pointing students to the relevant aspects of their Activity Guide so they can see these terms concretely. For test data, consider pointing students to the dots on the chart in their Activity Guide as an example."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d7e0844-2e6b-4433-af73-4d30e408629d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60cca803-97f1-4eb1-ac97-6c0207a1748d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *loss function*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60cca803-97f1-4eb1-ac97-6c0207a1748d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f46f0608-11ae-4680-aeae-5a48d8244361",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "*Loss function* is just a formal word for what we’ve been talking about already: a loss function describes how we should calculate a model’s loss to help a model learn to get better. There are many different loss functions—that is, ways to calculate a model’s loss. We’ve been talking about one in particular…",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider pointing students to the place they calculated loss in their Activity Guide. What they did in that section represents a loss function: they chose a way to calculate a model’s loss."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f46f0608-11ae-4680-aeae-5a48d8244361",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9804db1e-6c33-4cc3-b341-f2f891129b60",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *absolute error*. Indicate that absolute error is one possible loss function."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9804db1e-6c33-4cc3-b341-f2f891129b60",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6a7f2ef-7593-4109-8e5d-62a686bf4bbb",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Absolute error is one way to calculate a model’s loss. It simply looks at the distance between what a model should have predicted and what it actually predicted. Absolute error is a loss function. It’s the one we’ll implement in code next.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider pointing students to the Prediction and Actual values in the table of their Activity Guide. Absolute error describes calculating the distance between the Prediction and Actual values. Point out that distance can never be negative. A prediction of 1 and 3 are the same distance from the actual value 2: that distance is 1."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6a7f2ef-7593-4109-8e5d-62a686bf4bbb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9a7054bd-6b91-41b3-952e-2b5a65ee00aa",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Invite students to put their loss-calculation thinking into Python code, then use it to compare two models that make predictions about data of their choice.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Comparing Models"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9a7054bd-6b91-41b3-952e-2b5a65ee00aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1fe3e7bc-4168-4d6e-bf44-ac074477e8fb",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Writing a Loss Function"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1fe3e7bc-4168-4d6e-bf44-ac074477e8fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b4e9eac-dbee-4b7d-9109-892f237f00c5",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Levels 1–2.\n- **Level 1:** Students use AI to implement an absolute error function in Python code. They see how their loss function is used to compare two different models.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks them to choose which model in Level 1 was better, and to express their thinking in terms of their loss function.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, check on their comfort with loss functions and with translating their ideas into code.\n- Some students may be stuck on how to prompt AI. Have them say aloud how to calculate absolute error, then type that same explanation as their prompt.\n- Some students might have AI-generated code that doesn’t match what they’ve described. Prompt students to read their code back: “Does this do what you wanted? How can you tell?”",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 as an assessment opportunity to check whether students can define what makes one regression model better than another, and whether they can express their thinking in terms of the loss they calculated. Look for answers that both correctly identify the better model and describe its lower loss as a reason it’s better. If students misidentify the better model, or fail to bring loss calculation into their rationale, consider prompting students to explain their loss function in their own words, pointing out how it helps them judge which model is better."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b4e9eac-dbee-4b7d-9109-892f237f00c5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7f1fe8b8-9ffb-4731-875b-d4a542c6109f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4fb52b4-5642-4311-8212-661a54499773",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "A common answer to this question might be: “We can only know relative to the loss function we’re using.” This answer might open up further questions: how do we choose a good loss function? If you’re feeling comfortable and there’s additional time, consider facilitating a discussion around this question. If the question feels too advanced for now, it will be explored in more depth in Unit 4."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4fb52b4-5642-4311-8212-661a54499773",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3aebda17-a3e5-475d-a765-c6f54540abdb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fca92710-2110-4dff-9369-2c9442a94fa5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fca92710-2110-4dff-9369-2c9442a94fa5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3aebda17-a3e5-475d-a765-c6f54540abdb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59704d35-894b-40df-8f2b-ca2ef8fc9dd8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Anita’s ice cream shop is trying to predict how many customers she will have, so that she can order the right amount of ingredients each month.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59704d35-894b-40df-8f2b-ca2ef8fc9dd8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3997b99d-71b2-4e37-8ebc-26cb2cdb5310"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "350d79ec-3377-48c2-8125-459c40628b8a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students for a check of their intuitions: do they think Anita will have more customers in the summer months or in the winter months?\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "350d79ec-3377-48c2-8125-459c40628b8a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3997b99d-71b2-4e37-8ebc-26cb2cdb5310"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9021609f-d7ea-407e-8013-9988b4b3e4d5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Anita found this model online and is using it to make predictions.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9021609f-d7ea-407e-8013-9988b4b3e4d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3997b99d-71b2-4e37-8ebc-26cb2cdb5310"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a09be754-e25b-4faf-aecf-9deae03ce4aa",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pose questions to students inviting them to interpret the model: Approximately how much ice cream does the model predict will sell in July? How much ice cream does the model predict for January? Students should interpret the model and conclude that the model predicts that Anita will sell around 100 units of ice cream in July and 400 in January.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *It seems like the model is a good fit for the data, but its predictions don’t make sense. Why do you think that could be?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider their own intuitions behind why a model can be a good fit for training data and yet still make unexpected or incorrect predictions. Strong responses might speculate that there’s a problem with the training data. Students might suggest that there should be more training data to help the model make better predictions, or that the training data is different from Anita’s scenario in some way. Move on once a few different ideas have been surfaced."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a09be754-e25b-4faf-aecf-9deae03ce4aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3997b99d-71b2-4e37-8ebc-26cb2cdb5310"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "197ae65b-cd5f-4960-b673-21596bc375e4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It turns out that the model Anita found was built using data from an ice cream shop in Australia, where the seasons are flipped: it’s hotter in January than in July, so people buy more ice cream in January than in July. The model is a good fit for the data that it was trained on, but that data isn’t an accurate representation of the context that Anita is using the model in. A model is only as good as the data it’s trained on.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "197ae65b-cd5f-4960-b673-21596bc375e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3997b99d-71b2-4e37-8ebc-26cb2cdb5310"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fb5dcdb6-18ca-4237-9710-42a5ea0c7a23",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _biased data_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fb5dcdb6-18ca-4237-9710-42a5ea0c7a23",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3997b99d-71b2-4e37-8ebc-26cb2cdb5310"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86b143ba-a136-4b60-99cc-c9393df70433",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The dataset Anita used was biased, because Australian seasons don’t match the real-world situation her shop is in.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86b143ba-a136-4b60-99cc-c9393df70433",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3997b99d-71b2-4e37-8ebc-26cb2cdb5310"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1afe7e2c-6dca-499c-bd3e-91121abc1fa8",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _representative data_.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1afe7e2c-6dca-499c-bd3e-91121abc1fa8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3997b99d-71b2-4e37-8ebc-26cb2cdb5310"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25f0c564-375e-4eee-9c3c-f1b325308eea",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "For Anita’s dataset to be representative, it needed to include data from shops like hers.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25f0c564-375e-4eee-9c3c-f1b325308eea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3997b99d-71b2-4e37-8ebc-26cb2cdb5310"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aef40139-14ec-4e9e-be7e-f62bd195d847",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the question of the day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aef40139-14ec-4e9e-be7e-f62bd195d847",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3997b99d-71b2-4e37-8ebc-26cb2cdb5310"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eaa4fb10-e051-4ce2-a292-3f311b35f9c7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Invite students to evaluate datasets for whether they are biased or representative.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Exploring Data Bias"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eaa4fb10-e051-4ce2-a292-3f311b35f9c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "30fa3e9f-5b56-48f9-8c39-c270dc9ff6b3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In order to determine whether or not the data we’re building a model with is representative, we need to understand both the data and the context where the model will be used. Here are some questions that can help us evaluate the data.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "30fa3e9f-5b56-48f9-8c39-c270dc9ff6b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c3ba54e-dac1-4d4e-9ddc-6e40da6f2fa4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read aloud the questions for determining representativeness: What group are we trying to make predictions about? What are the important subgroups within that group? Does the data include each relevant subgroup?\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c3ba54e-dac1-4d4e-9ddc-6e40da6f2fa4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "195c7bdb-7b95-4481-98b3-418350039412",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the Anita’s case, we were trying to make predictions about ice cream buyers. Ice cream buyers could be from lots of different places around the world, but the dataset only included data about ice cream buyers in Australia.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This heuristic is a simplification around biased data and doesn’t account for datasets that include insufficient data from particular subgroups. That requires understanding sampling and sample sizes, which are introduced in Unit 3, Lesson 10.  Students who are familiar with issues of data bias might point this out or raise examples in the discussion questions that relate to disproportionate sampling. If that happens feel free to explain the simplification and mention that the details will be explored in future sessions.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "195c7bdb-7b95-4481-98b3-418350039412",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a843b6f-134e-4262-b705-ef25e25d75b3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair of student a copy of the Exploring Data Bias Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Exploring Data Bias Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the activity guide, help them think through the three questions to determine whether a dataset is representative. For students who are stuck, prompt them by asking them to think about any groups of people who aren’t included in the table.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students take more time on the activity than expected, it’s okay to stop the activity after the first two exercises rather than completing all three.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a843b6f-134e-4262-b705-ef25e25d75b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62a52518-57f5-42ae-a42b-cb570a5721fb",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The examples we’ve been looking at have fairly low stakes: if Anita accidentally buys too much ice cream mix for July, that likely won’t directly harm someone. In other situations, though, models trained on biased data can result in predictions or decisions that are harmful to the unrepresented group.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62a52518-57f5-42ae-a42b-cb570a5721fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e7388cb-c46b-4853-b530-344844b9d271",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What undesirable outcomes can occur when you use a model that was trained on biased data? Can you think of examples where a biased model might make harmful or unfair predictions?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Explore the real world implications of using models that are trained on biased data. Strong responses will articulate how the decisions made by the model trained on biased data may be incorrect when the model is used. If students struggle to come up with ideas, suggest some possible real-world examples of machine learning and ask them what the implications of a biased decision might be. Strong examples that students might surface include:\n- Trying to determine whether or not a student should be admitted to your college: if data doesn’t include a particular group (like non-native English speakers, or students of particular ethnicities) the model might make unfair decisions when it encounters people from that group.\n- Facial recognition software trained only on images of white faces (or disproportionately on images of white faces, if students are familiar with the topic) might not recognise or correctly categorise other faces – this may result in non-white people experiencing issues with software that uses face-recognition technology to control access.\n- In the Activity, students may point out that it is problematic to try to predict how points a basketball player will score as if it is independent of the competition they play against, and that a more effective models would need to make predictions based on the expected competition. If not, consider bringing it up as a specific prompt. In either case, noting that all the examples we’ve seen so far are relatively simple to help us learn the core concepts."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e7388cb-c46b-4853-b530-344844b9d271",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02502b05-abd0-4b80-87bf-6735eb461432",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students are introduced to lists as a data structure for storing a sequence of values. They look at lists in the context of data sets for machine learning models and analyze those lists for representativeness.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Working with Lists"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02502b05-abd0-4b80-87bf-6735eb461432",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d0ec169-292c-4e76-85a4-b03c39918407",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When working with data, we need some way to represent multiple values together in code.\n\nPython has a data type called a list that can store a sequence of values.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d0ec169-292c-4e76-85a4-b03c39918407",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb8a1796-449f-429b-940f-30f1ea9b9c9e",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the syntax for a Python list. Lists begin with a square bracket, end with a square bracket, and use commas to separate items in the sequence.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb8a1796-449f-429b-940f-30f1ea9b9c9e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce6dea42-f55e-41ef-8538-03b7b0013fea",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In Python, we can use a `for` loop to loop through a list one value at a time. Inside the loop, we can do something with each value.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce6dea42-f55e-41ef-8538-03b7b0013fea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4034b06e-1104-4bea-9e67-8ebc2209b2b9",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example of looping through the list and printing each value. Ask students what they think the output of the program will be.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the console output and explain that each value is printed, one at a time.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4034b06e-1104-4bea-9e67-8ebc2209b2b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7302fff8-72b4-4dc1-b84e-f89a7fe23d83",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When writing Python programs to train machine learning models, it’s helpful to store data in a list. Today, you’ll practice working with lists and analyze the data stored in them.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7302fff8-72b4-4dc1-b84e-f89a7fe23d83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ae01e716-eb8f-450e-8425-1b19147866a5",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Analyzing Data in Lists"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ae01e716-eb8f-450e-8425-1b19147866a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b8c45c1-a692-4086-8910-9a755317ebc0",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1–3.\n- Level 1: Students write a loop that iterates through a list.\n- Level 2: Students wrote code to filter values in a list and print values that belong to different categories. They use the results to assess whether a dataset is representative.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks them to evaluate the representativeness of a dataset and explain why representativeness is important.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, help students with common issues they might run into when working with lists for the first time.\n- Some students might struggle with the syntax for looping over a list. Remind them of the structure of a `for` loop, pointing out that they can give the loop variable a name of their own choice.\n- Some students might struggle with conditionally printing items in a loop. Help students think through the different pieces of logic involved: they can loop through each value one at a time, use an `if` statement to check whether each value satisfies a particular condition, then perform logic like printing a value inside the if statement. To do this, students should nest their conditional statement inside of the loop body.\n- Some students might have trouble checking for multiple conditions at once (for example, checking if a value is greater than one number but less than another number). The level’s coding tip offers students a suggestion for how to perform this check.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 as an assessment opportunity to check whether students can articulate the reasons why a particular dataset might not be representative—for example, it might be missing data from a particular subgroup of the population.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b8c45c1-a692-4086-8910-9a755317ebc0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "13e30333-b5f3-442b-b190-6dc049843733"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a23feda-02a9-4b22-99cd-059c5a0a0ab8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the question of the day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a23feda-02a9-4b22-99cd-059c5a0a0ab8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7af91a6-ba68-4be7-9e5c-30a697604c8c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "093a8284-dfaf-49f9-9719-32ba3e4ec65e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s Career Journeys connection."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "093a8284-dfaf-49f9-9719-32ba3e4ec65e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24808930-ab41-47f4-b557-8e2dc535491e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e0efaf4a-d237-4f91-84c6-e7006385adac",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’ll see today how AI is used to make predictions about the important work of delivering mail on time.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e0efaf4a-d237-4f91-84c6-e7006385adac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24808930-ab41-47f4-b557-8e2dc535491e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6125baf7-1280-4555-9476-2f0b82d42e53",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you know—or not know—about how mail gets delivered in your community?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Invite students’ voices and experiences into this lesson at its start. Some students may know very little about how mail gets delivered, while others may have family or family-friends who work in the postal service. If students don’t know much about how mail gets delivered, prompt them to express what they don’t know as questions: “Who delivers the mail?”, “How would I send a letter, if I wanted to?”, or “What happens if mail gets lost?” If students do know something about how mail gets delivered—maybe they know about their local postal worker, have been to a post office, or have seen a mail truck—invite them to share what they do know. Once you’ve drawn out students’ connections to mail and mail delivery, feel free to start the lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6125baf7-1280-4555-9476-2f0b82d42e53",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24808930-ab41-47f4-b557-8e2dc535491e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "363c3213-9eb0-43b8-9a36-c20a53391e94",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Through a hands-on activity, students come to see and understand the nearest neighbors classification algorithm.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Narrate the context for the Nearest Neighbors Activity Guide.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Exploring Nearest Neighbors"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "363c3213-9eb0-43b8-9a36-c20a53391e94",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3015048f-36e0-453e-80a6-5ae6484c273f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Postmasters often want to know whether a day’s mail will arrive late or on time. This is an example of a *classification* problem: we need to predict a label.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3015048f-36e0-453e-80a6-5ae6484c273f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "209111bb-5417-4435-a62a-5a716ec3fe81",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *label*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "209111bb-5417-4435-a62a-5a716ec3fe81",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4cd22996-238c-43f5-861d-dde2e81c0865",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this problem, we have two possible labels: ‘Late’ or ‘On Time.’ In other classification problems, we could have more than two possible labels.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4cd22996-238c-43f5-861d-dde2e81c0865",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b0213c0-c3f0-4858-86c2-2eff3cb40000",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To make a prediction, we can look at features: pieces of information about each day.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b0213c0-c3f0-4858-86c2-2eff3cb40000",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f411e183-3ddb-4708-8769-8fcf06cb3aa5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *feature*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f411e183-3ddb-4708-8769-8fcf06cb3aa5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd54f842-6c02-413b-a0e4-e64037715ae7",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In your activity guide, you’ll use snowfall to predict whether a day’s mail will be late or on time.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd54f842-6c02-413b-a0e4-e64037715ae7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e1983f02-a672-4164-8dd7-01025773cd61",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *classifier*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e1983f02-a672-4164-8dd7-01025773cd61",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7ec4046-776b-49a5-b570-76bf2f9efbf3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ll explore a specific kind of classifier today: a nearest-neighbors classifier.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7ec4046-776b-49a5-b570-76bf2f9efbf3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9909a223-c397-499a-8dad-f04b0c0e5b47",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Nearest Neighbors Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Nearest Neighbors Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, watch how they find neighbors. Look for students roughly measuring distance along the line to identify the closest day(s), rather than guessing from the snowfall amount alone. For Three-Nearest Neighbors, check that students take the most common label among the three closest days, rather than defaulting to the single closest. If students are stuck, ask: \"Which past day is closest to Today?\" and \"What label does that day have?\" For three neighbors, ask: \"Of the three closest days, which label shows up most often?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does a nearest neighbors classifier work? Can you think of additional features, beyond snowfall, that would help us predict whether a day’s mail will arrive on time?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Check for students’ understanding of the key ideas behind the Nearest Neighbors Activity Guide: that the nearest neighbors classification algorithm labels new data by looking at the labels of existing data with similar features. Strong responses will describe how, by finding similar data it already knows about, a computer can arrive at a prediction for data it’s never seen before. If students struggle to address how nearest neighbors works, ask them to put into words what they did in the Activity Guide. Follow up by asking students to imagine other features of a day, beyond snowfall, that might be useful for determining whether the mail will be delivered on time. Encourage students to think of things that are unexpected: significant rainfall, low visibility, slippery roads, and more. Strong ideas are both 1) measurable, and 2) likely to matter for delivering mail on time."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9909a223-c397-499a-8dad-f04b0c0e5b47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6298d8a-47f3-41de-9240-a4b950d8bb24",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Build on students’ hands-on understanding of nearest neighbors. Introduce them to nearest neighbors with two features, and to the ways features and their values can be represented in code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to how to represent features and their values in programs.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Nearest Neighbors Classification"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6298d8a-47f3-41de-9240-a4b950d8bb24",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f928ac90-4ea6-4149-9899-bd8ed83b4a4a",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you add more features, you’ll need a way to organize this data for a computer. Python offers something called a dictionary to do just that.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f928ac90-4ea6-4149-9899-bd8ed83b4a4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1e9f806-b0a8-4548-a2a5-c8b7b612d860",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect the visual representation of features to the Python representation of a dictionary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1e9f806-b0a8-4548-a2a5-c8b7b612d860",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bf1f1a90-62a7-42ec-bda3-2b9a51f7a032",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "On the left, we see two features: snowfall and visibility. Each has their own value. On the right, we see a Python dictionary. The dictionary is called today. In Python, a dictionary is created with curly brackets: { and }. Inside the curly brackets are pairs of keys and values. The keys, on the left, represent the names of features—like snowfall and visibility. The values, on the right, represent the values of those features. As you can see, a dictionary is a common way to group related information.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bf1f1a90-62a7-42ec-bda3-2b9a51f7a032",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa54083c-c5bb-40f8-8cc4-e3d953439099",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Nearest Neighbors Classification"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa54083c-c5bb-40f8-8cc4-e3d953439099",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bddabb83-2139-4507-a1cf-43b5956fbe4e",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1–4.\n- **Level 1:** Students choose a context in which to make predictions about whether mail will be delivered on time. They run a program that plots, in two dimensions, data about previous deliveries. They then see a nearest neighbors classifier’s prediction about whether a new date’s mail will be late or on time.\n- **Level 2:**  Students edit the values of the new date’s features, assessing how the model’s prediction changes.\n- **Level 3:**  Students change how many neighbors the nearest neighbors classifier uses to make a prediction. They try to find an optimal number of neighbors to consider.\n- **Level 4:**  Students complete a Check Your Understanding that prompts them to describe their findings from the previous levels.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students move through the levels, offer guidance in these ways:\n- **Level 1:** Check whether students can locate the model's prediction on the plot: it appears in the title and along the dashed lines connecting Today to the neighbors it used.\n- **Level 2:** Check whether students can connect changing a feature's value to the prediction changing. Prompt them to notice how moving Today toward one group of points changes which neighbors are closest.\n- **Level 3:** Look for students who assume more neighbors is always better; encourage them to try several values and notice when the prediction stays stable versus when it flips. If students are unsure what to look at, ask: \"Where does the plot show the prediction?\", \"Which points are connected to Today, and why those?\", and \"What happens to the prediction as you change the number of neighbors?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 4 can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives. Look for most students explaining that a nearest neighbors classifier predicts a label by finding the most similar past example(s). Also look for evidence that students have tried multiple variations on the nearest neighbors approach, changing the number of neighbors considered. If students struggle to reason about the number of neighbors, point them back to Level 3 and have them compare predictions from a small number of neighbors (e.g., 2–3), and from an (overly) large number of neighbors (e.g., 10+). Prompt them to notice that more neighbors aren’t always better: at some point, you start to lose the signal in the noise."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bddabb83-2139-4507-a1cf-43b5956fbe4e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8b6307b6-4f16-4bce-982a-47cbec0aa9ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f23c1ec-a86d-43fe-9872-465d1125f931",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f23c1ec-a86d-43fe-9872-465d1125f931",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29be8c48-0b25-4385-a9f5-70ff8dff235b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8269fa88-066b-4312-970a-f1884816b729",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s Career Journeys connection."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8269fa88-066b-4312-970a-f1884816b729",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4410c847-36ac-4048-ad6b-2761380dae8a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "032daee0-328e-44fb-b068-4e0747cf3279",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we’re exploring how classifiers can help rangers and other naturalists better identify the plants and animals around them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "032daee0-328e-44fb-b068-4e0747cf3279",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4410c847-36ac-4048-ad6b-2761380dae8a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21a46627-7f06-4a2d-ba7f-756dcd6a238b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What plants or animals can you recognize in your neighborhood? How can you tell what they are?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Invite students’ voices and experiences into this lesson at its start. The primary goal of this discussion is simply to understand the kinds of plants and animals students are familiar with. Strong responses will name a living thing and the features that help identify it: its color, shape, size, sounds, where it grows, etc. If students can name a plant or animal they recognize, but struggle to identify how they can tell what it is, ask them questions as if you didn’t know how to identify it yourself: “If you had to tell me how to spot one, how would you do it?” In their response, try to identify the features—color, sound, smell, etc.—that make it identifiable. Connect the discussion to the day’s lesson, which is about how we can test classifiers that identify plants and animals.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21a46627-7f06-4a2d-ba7f-756dcd6a238b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4410c847-36ac-4048-ad6b-2761380dae8a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ffc005b2-d46b-4aab-9177-41ccfa09be5f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Invite students to explore three different metrics with which to evaluate classifiers: accuracy, recall, and precision.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the context in which students compare classifiers, and prompt students to pick a berry.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal which berry is toxic.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Comparing Classifiers",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students this scenario is just a classroom exercise, not real advice about berries to eat. In fact, elderberries—the berries labeled \"Not Toxic\"—are still safest when cooked, rather than eaten raw. Remind students that wild berries are difficult to identify, so as a general rule, it’s best not to eat them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ffc005b2-d46b-4aab-9177-41ccfa09be5f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80141961-230f-4eaa-bc3e-55bd2b7d4ed2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A ranger has built two classifiers to help people identify toxic berries. It’ll be up to you to test them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80141961-230f-4eaa-bc3e-55bd2b7d4ed2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "03613036-a695-4297-8b0c-10202b4079ff",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Berry Picking Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the first part of the Berry Picking Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students fill in the accuracy rows, look for students to compare each model’s predictions (in the Model A and Model B columns) with the correct prediction (in the Actual column). If students need guidance, consider prompting them to look at one row at a time: “For this row, did Model A’s prediction match the Actual column?” Have them tally their yeses. Finally, confirm students arrive at 70% accuracy for Model A and 80% accuracy for Model B before moving on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *accuracy*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "03613036-a695-4297-8b0c-10202b4079ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7c786211-5206-46d5-bfbd-cbee0fe28ade",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you’ve seen, accuracy is one way to assess how well a classifier works.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7c786211-5206-46d5-bfbd-cbee0fe28ade",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2db0b4e2-440f-45ca-96c2-b4750b36e264",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students which classifier from the Activity Guide they think is better. Students may vote for Model B, because its accuracy is higher. But as the activity continues, they may change their minds.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the rest of the Berry Picking Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Keep in mind that measures of recall and precision can be more difficult to grasp than accuracy: they rely on different denominators. For recall, it’s important to ensure students look only at the model’s predictions for rows that are actually toxic (i.e., the first 5 rows) and ask how many of those the model correctly identified as toxic. For precision, it’s important students look only at the rows where the model predicted the berries to be toxic and ask how many were right. Listen for students who are looking beyond these rows, and guide them back to the definition of recall and precision.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *recall*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2db0b4e2-440f-45ca-96c2-b4750b36e264",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2f1f31e-d29e-4867-b89e-90c5de635f35",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In our activity, recall represents the proportion of actually toxic berries the model identified. That is, looking only at the berries that are actually toxic, recall is the proportion the model identified as toxic.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Check that students found Model A to have a recall of 5/5 (100%) and Model B to have a recall of 3/5 (60%). If not, step through the calculation in the Activity Guide."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2f1f31e-d29e-4867-b89e-90c5de635f35",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b1c92d5-d74e-4d98-9f48-647d7e1de76c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *precision*.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b1c92d5-d74e-4d98-9f48-647d7e1de76c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b52a43a-1f80-4395-9ee1-b6653a5fe0e7",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In our activity, precision represents the proportion of berries the model thought were toxic that were actually toxic. That is, looking only at the berries the model predicted are toxic, precision is the proportion of those predictions the model got correct.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Check that students found Model A to have a precision of 5/8 (63%) and Model B to have a precision of 3/3 (100%). If not, step through the calculation in the Activity Guide."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b52a43a-1f80-4395-9ee1-b6653a5fe0e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fdcb9ffc-1be6-43b8-95b7-3b2d92ac6093",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which model did you recommend? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to see that each metric—accuracy, recall, and precision—tells a different story about a model’s performance, and that a model can only be effectively evaluated by looking at all three. Strong responses will emphasize that, for a model meant to keep people from eating something toxic, it’s especially important to prioritize recall: the model’s ability to identify a toxic berry as a toxic berry. While this same model may perform worse on other measures, these mistakes are less costly than saying a toxic berry is safe to eat. Consider guiding students towards this line of thinking by arguing, hypothetically, that because one model has a higher accuracy than the other, it’s a better model. Prompt students to critique this reasoning. End the discussion by emphasizing that the right metric to prioritize depends on the goal and the cost of making certain kinds of mistakes."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fdcb9ffc-1be6-43b8-95b7-3b2d92ac6093",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b2b97b5-2ffc-4396-98e0-0377760e6d93",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’ve seen how the best way to evaluate a classifier depends significantly on its goals. In our next activity, you’ll learn how you can tune a classifier to prioritize different goals.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b2b97b5-2ffc-4396-98e0-0377760e6d93",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a18d2dc9-d363-494c-9200-78471c2d16c7",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Help students see that a classifier doesn’t just output a binary prediction, like “toxic” or “not toxic.” Instead, it often produces a probability-based confidence. A confidence threshold is one parameter students can tweak to optimize a model’s recall and precision for a given context.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define confidence.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Provide examples of confidence scores and what they mean.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Should a classifier say this berry is, in fact, toxic?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to apply their understanding of confidence scores to a situation they’re, by now, familiar with. There is no right answer, but the process of taking a side can reinforce students’ understanding of confidence scores and of how different mistakes can have different costs. Strong responses will reference the costs of making a mistake: “Yes, the classifier thinks it’s more likely toxic than not and, even if it’s not completely sure, it would be bad if it said the berry wasn’t toxic when it was.” End the discussion by previewing the next idea about thresholds: that the class has essentially been arguing where to draw the line between when a model predicts “toxic” vs. “not toxic.”",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Tuning Classifiers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a18d2dc9-d363-494c-9200-78471c2d16c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6070ebc-9c58-4368-95b7-8e21688c1357",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "After a model calculates its confidence, it might still need to make a claim about whether the berry is toxic or not toxic. Let’s see how a model might decide this.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6070ebc-9c58-4368-95b7-8e21688c1357",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74dda293-1718-49f8-905c-65be3e72e14c",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define threshold using the meter on the slide: the confidence level at which a classifier will predict that a berry is toxic.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74dda293-1718-49f8-905c-65be3e72e14c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a09e3d61-8ef8-4368-84f3-ea5971f83f21",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If the classifier’s confidence is above the threshold, it will say this berry is toxic.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a09e3d61-8ef8-4368-84f3-ea5971f83f21",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab87173b-1033-4c23-948f-8421749436d3",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If the classifier’s confidence is below the threshold, it won’t.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab87173b-1033-4c23-948f-8421749436d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1404f84-14ef-4d4e-b8ba-e03658c0f1b8",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Where to place this threshold is an important question—both in this problem and in others!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1404f84-14ef-4d4e-b8ba-e03658c0f1b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ea7fe5c-f554-4675-800b-4adc5c9b1ec5",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Tuning Classifiers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ea7fe5c-f554-4675-800b-4adc5c9b1ec5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "225e94e3-e896-496a-bfa6-3ab33c46aca0",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1–2.\n- **Level 1:** Students explore an interactive widget in which they tune a classifier in a context of their choice. They aim to find the optimal threshold for the context they’ve chosen.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding that prompts them to reflect on their experience and justify the threshold they chose.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students explore the widget, look for the realization that a lower threshold raises recall but lowers precision. Prompt students to experiment with both very low and very high thresholds, asking them to pay attention to what happens to the recall and precision metrics in particular.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 2 can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives. Look for students to justify their threshold by naming the relevant metric they prioritized and offering relevant reasoning about why they chose it. Strong responses may mention the cost of different mistakes: in some contexts, it may be costly to have a low recall, while in other contexts, it may be costly to have a low precision."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "225e94e3-e896-496a-bfa6-3ab33c46aca0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25a21684-ea1c-4529-9bd5-ebeb37e5fbed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df5a35fb-600b-4797-806f-5c2ce7f3a4c9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df5a35fb-600b-4797-806f-5c2ce7f3a4c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e9b77cf0-7079-4869-8a62-b3bbc8a57a85"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2fdc287-9cae-4c92-af9c-cc17413bda9d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Machine learning helps computers sense, and make sense of, the world around us. Today, we’ll explore how computers can learn to \"see\" and \"hear.\" Let’s look at an example of an autonomous robot, called Atlas, which seems to sense the world around itself much like a human would.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2fdc287-9cae-4c92-af9c-cc17413bda9d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef50655a-43c8-4d95-8df3-a469d0e241b7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ae074cfb-fcd9-44f1-baa6-90408f135da4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt to think to themselves as they watch: “What senses would Atlas need to navigate the world around it?”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Atlas Gets a Grip” video.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What senses do you think Atlas needs to sense the world around it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface students’ thoughts after watching the video. Strong responses will relate the tasks Atlas performed—placing a wooden board, grabbing a bag, doing a backflip—with senses that help perform them: sight, touch, balance, and more. If students aren’t sure, consider prompting them with a follow-up question: “What senses would be helpful for you to perform the tasks you saw Atlas perform?” Once students have named a few senses they think Atlas might have, move on to the next part of the lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *multimodal model*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ae074cfb-fcd9-44f1-baa6-90408f135da4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef50655a-43c8-4d95-8df3-a469d0e241b7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6857c94d-57f9-4946-8816-49f1ef3295f2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today is all about how AI systems can sense the world in many different ways. We’ll focus in particular on images and sound.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6857c94d-57f9-4946-8816-49f1ef3295f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef50655a-43c8-4d95-8df3-a469d0e241b7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dabef32d-e963-4d70-b04c-df094e47422e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dabef32d-e963-4d70-b04c-df094e47422e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef50655a-43c8-4d95-8df3-a469d0e241b7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44a6b6b4-ae21-4d36-a5b3-24b73898cc74",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** By exploring how computers ‘see,’ students learn that computers don’t sense the world in the same way humans do: rather, they sense the world through numeric representations.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use what students saw in the \"Atlas Gets a Grip\" video to motivate an exploration of how computers see.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Seeing"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44a6b6b4-ae21-4d36-a5b3-24b73898cc74",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ad6193f0-2caa-4911-abd9-14af96807358",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A robot like Atlas might seem to see the world in a human-like way. But humans and computers see the same world in very different ways.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ad6193f0-2caa-4911-abd9-14af96807358",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16223719-4390-44ff-a69b-e4e7e50811d9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A computer has no eyes. It has only numbers: specifically, ones and zeros called bits. Humans and computers see the same world in very different ways.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16223719-4390-44ff-a69b-e4e7e50811d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f54cd4ad-92b2-41e2-a456-03c9a6dd207f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to find a partner for the Seeing Through Bits Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student pair a copy of the Seeing Through Bits Activity Guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "There are five available variations of the Activity Guide, each revealing a different image. If you want groups to reveal different images, give different groups different variations."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f54cd4ad-92b2-41e2-a456-03c9a6dd207f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0613c43-9722-42a3-8849-716c10bf59f4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Seeing Through Bits Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students complete the Activity Guide, look for pairs working together to find and fill in the 1’s in the grid. Keep a close eye on students who accidentally misread the instructions—1’s should be colored in black, while 0’s should be white.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the five possible images students could have revealed (one for each variation of the Activity Guide).\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0613c43-9722-42a3-8849-716c10bf59f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1557e5dc-b179-441c-80fe-497a83d494a4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Though a computer can only 'see' numbers, those numbers can represent something about the world, like light or colors. Computers can then use those numbers to sense the world.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1557e5dc-b179-441c-80fe-497a83d494a4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7b6fc19-88ab-40cf-b697-8f54857dbf01",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define representation."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7b6fc19-88ab-40cf-b697-8f54857dbf01",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c96ee3a-81f0-4110-9df0-e7624d9248ee",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember that in this example, the sequence of numbers represents an entire image.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c96ee3a-81f0-4110-9df0-e7624d9248ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd2bae67-63a6-43ef-a492-bde915dd1a44",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What patterns could a computer find in your numbers to recognize what the image is?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Connect the idea of representation to machine learning: when a machine learns to identify something in an image, it’s really learning to find a pattern of numbers that represent that thing. Students don’t need to have specific answers—rather, answers that convey the general idea of recognizing lines, circles, and other patterns suffice to get the point across. Once students have the sense that a computer is looking for patterns in numbers, rather than in some sense of sight, continue on with the Activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the takeaway sentence in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd2bae67-63a6-43ef-a492-bde915dd1a44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e41c656-9111-479f-a6f5-f7334d085951",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** By exploring how computers ‘hear,’ students come to see that humans choose how to represent the world in terms a computer can understand. They also see that these choices impact how well a computer can sense the world.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the next activity.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Hearing"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e41c656-9111-479f-a6f5-f7334d085951",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a449c461-f671-4eea-8241-1a2d432bd8fc",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a moment, you’ll hear two different instruments. One of them is a cello, and the other is a violin.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a449c461-f671-4eea-8241-1a2d432bd8fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c83fe32-5767-4999-8c2e-29404a0e7628",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Can you guess which is a cello and which is a violin?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c83fe32-5767-4999-8c2e-29404a0e7628",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34c5d0c2-cd36-45a1-a415-2bb43c6f4580",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Play each of the notes. Let students vote for which is the cello. Then, let students vote for which is the violin.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal which note was played by a cello, and which by a violin. If students aren’t very familiar with cellos and violins, offer a brief explanation of the difference. Both are string instruments, but a cello is a much larger (and deeper-sounding) instrument.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "It’s not expected every student know the difference between a cello and a violin. If some students seem confidently correct, or perhaps even play the cello or violin themselves, consider inviting them to share what they know with others."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34c5d0c2-cd36-45a1-a415-2bb43c6f4580",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2fc26a19-f34d-4826-b4c5-7cf91889562c",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Suppose we want a computer to \"hear\" a sound and figure out what instrument made it. What would be helpful for a computer to sense in order to do so?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to identify features of the instruments that distinguish them. As students bring up ideas, ask them to think about how they would measure these features numerically, going back to the idea that computers really only understand numbers."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2fc26a19-f34d-4826-b4c5-7cf91889562c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1617a338-e737-4fce-8444-dafad52db611",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s one way a computer can 'hear' sound. These are spectrograms of each note. Spectrograms visualize how loud a sound’s frequencies are over time. 'Frequency' means how high- or low-pitched a sound is. In a spectrogram, louder frequencies are darker. Quieter frequencies are lighter.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1617a338-e737-4fce-8444-dafad52db611",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42ca7b82-e749-4452-9516-7340403e6aa1",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What differences do you see between the spectrograms of the cello and the violin?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students toward the realization that—generally speaking—violins tend to play higher notes, and thus have a higher frequency, than cellos. It’s okay if students don’t use precise language: they could say, for instance, that the cello’s spectrogram is “darker on the bottom” or the violin’s is “darker toward the top.” Try to interpret students’ imprecise language more precisely: you could respond by saying, “You’re right, the cello does have a darker part on the bottom. That means this cello is playing at a lower frequency, which means it will sound lower pitched to us.”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Emphasize that, to translate sound into something a computer can understand, humans make choices about what features of the sound to capture numerically."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42ca7b82-e749-4452-9516-7340403e6aa1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa3f33cc-03fe-4b3c-bce9-71585faa48c1",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To represent each note, we’ll need to choose the features we think are most important for the computer to sense.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa3f33cc-03fe-4b3c-bce9-71585faa48c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c83767d1-9329-4ec3-b2a2-584242f27cda",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Preview how students will work with these features in Python code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c83767d1-9329-4ec3-b2a2-584242f27cda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86169518-afca-4f1a-9a55-462798be71a8",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In Python, we can represent each note as a dictionary with these features.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86169518-afca-4f1a-9a55-462798be71a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e37f3d5-3f16-4fa5-9bff-d96f2e7a092d",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember a dictionary has keys, like 'volume,' and 'frequency.'",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e37f3d5-3f16-4fa5-9bff-d96f2e7a092d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b7cbbcf-3554-44a2-a27c-ba2e85a36aa8",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "These keys have values, like 300 and 60.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b7cbbcf-3554-44a2-a27c-ba2e85a36aa8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "871a4cdc-538f-4135-8572-bb356bbd20be",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To access a value from a dictionary, use its key. Write the key’s name in square brackets after the dictionary’s name.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "871a4cdc-538f-4135-8572-bb356bbd20be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd7a67a3-2085-45c0-a584-a10ffc53f915",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, you’ll write a simple program to help a computer hear the difference between a violin and a cello.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd7a67a3-2085-45c0-a584-a10ffc53f915",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9691c10-6ea7-4e57-96db-59bad03fe8ad",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Choosing Representations (Part 1)"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9691c10-6ea7-4e57-96db-59bad03fe8ad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8cfb9c0a-fbd3-430e-9540-adc781b6fa84",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students write a simple conditional to classify cellos and violins by their frequency and volume. However, they should notice that their classifier doesn’t do very well when it can only sense frequency: sometimes, cellos and violins can play at the same frequency—for example, if they play the same note.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write Python code, look for:\n- **Level 1:** Help students use the new syntax they’ve learned to access the values of a Python dictionary. If necessary, prompt them to use AI tutor for assistance. In addition, guide students toward the realization that they could write a simple conditional to make a prediction based on a note’s frequency. Encourage students to try many variations on their conditional, testing their classifier many times.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Debrief student’s first attempt at a classifier that can only sense frequency and volume. Explain why frequency and volume aren’t enough to build a reliable classifier."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8cfb9c0a-fbd3-430e-9540-adc781b6fa84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d49026fb-45c7-45fa-bee5-db4ab3b1955f",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If a cello and violin play the same note, each will have the same frequency. And, neither instrument tends to be louder than the other.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d49026fb-45c7-45fa-bee5-db4ab3b1955f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b70a2fc2-257c-43be-93be-91d8024bd7a1",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Look again at the spectrograms of a cello and a violin. What else do you notice?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b70a2fc2-257c-43be-93be-91d8024bd7a1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef31e045-a283-4b76-bbc8-9864d1e31a68",
      "position": 28,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Guide students toward noticing that the violin’s spectrogram is darker overall, and that it has more visible bands of frequencies.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal that the violin’s spectrogram has more stacked bands of frequencies than the cello’s."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef31e045-a283-4b76-bbc8-9864d1e31a68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3cdbc690-04b2-43e5-b325-bd1f8a41f3be",
      "position": 29,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The violin’s spectrogram has many more visible, stacked bands of frequencies. The cello’s has fewer, fainter bands. More stacked bands of frequencies make an instrument sound brighter, not just higher-pitched. That makes sense for a violin: a violin is typically used to play bright melodies.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3cdbc690-04b2-43e5-b325-bd1f8a41f3be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ca1fa8f-08ec-471d-9d2b-d0231fa1a725",
      "position": 30,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Describe why choosing to add a brightness feature can help students build a better classifier."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ca1fa8f-08ec-471d-9d2b-d0231fa1a725",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efbd18b4-abee-43bd-8ac4-d41d5c88548b",
      "position": 31,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Choosing to add a brightness feature may help a computer tell apart the instruments. We know that violins will have a brighter sound than cellos.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efbd18b4-abee-43bd-8ac4-d41d5c88548b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05c69105-3c78-4670-bcd7-a225431c44b4",
      "position": 32,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, update your classifier to take brightness into account.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05c69105-3c78-4670-bcd7-a225431c44b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75823313-3dc7-4f9c-b8a2-4e300e927526",
      "position": 33,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Choosing Representations (Part 2)"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75823313-3dc7-4f9c-b8a2-4e300e927526",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "896cbe12-6be1-4d2b-92a5-19c1e9aecec6",
      "position": 34,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Levels 2–3.\n- **Level 2:** Students get access to a new feature, \"brightness.\" Students see that this feature, combined with frequency, helps their classifier better distinguish between cellos and violins.\n- **Level 3:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks them about why their classifier was improved by the addition of brightness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write Python code, look for:\n- **Level 2:** Encourage students to again explore many variations on their classifier, aiming to classify as many notes correctly as they can. If students tweak their classifier correctly, they’ll be able to successfully classify 90%+ of the notes. Also remind students they can use nested conditionals to take both brightness and frequency into account.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 3 can be used as a formative assessment of this Activity’s key takeaway: that the choices we make about how to represent the world determine how well a computer can make sense of it. Look for students describing how brightness has some predictive power when combined with frequency: we know that violins tend both to play higher notes and to sound brighter. When a computer has access to both of these senses, it’s able to make better predictions than with access to only one."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "896cbe12-6be1-4d2b-92a5-19c1e9aecec6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d21e5e7-2bd2-473e-86cc-5ef9865428f7",
      "position": 35,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the takeaway sentences in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d21e5e7-2bd2-473e-86cc-5ef9865428f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "99d21aa1-9cc5-420e-975b-533d122b02f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa8ad152-253d-4c96-a529-845f2019928b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa8ad152-253d-4c96-a529-845f2019928b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e2877047-ef1e-4fef-9cdd-d78cd29c76ba"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d900ef52-bab5-45f6-b274-4ec8975b4d09",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Highlight how pervasive machine learning technologies are and illustrate that they are used in contexts that students may not expect.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce “Spot the Machine Learning.”",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Spot the Machine Learning",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The examples get progressively more challenging, and the activity is designed to be fast-paced, so plan to take only one or two responses from your students before revealing the answer and moving on."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d900ef52-bab5-45f6-b274-4ec8975b4d09",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1860abd2-6ed5-47b5-8c6f-c98c2242498f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a59d995-65c7-48e5-ab81-582fbb46d1b5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the instructions to explain how the activity will unfold."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a59d995-65c7-48e5-ab81-582fbb46d1b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1860abd2-6ed5-47b5-8c6f-c98c2242498f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fe978d9-96aa-4dbf-bd95-4653472ec889",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Once I’ve described the scenario, raise your hand if you think you know the answer. We’ll start with an example.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fe978d9-96aa-4dbf-bd95-4653472ec889",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1860abd2-6ed5-47b5-8c6f-c98c2242498f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9c8ce8b-f6ec-4f73-8663-bb7051fbe79f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce an example scenario and its answer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9c8ce8b-f6ec-4f73-8663-bb7051fbe79f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1860abd2-6ed5-47b5-8c6f-c98c2242498f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7351f3d9-6813-421e-8263-ceb054511f3f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this example, a classification model is used to check if the face in front of the camera is you.  For the next examples, you have to identify where the ML is used and what’s being predicted. Ready?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7351f3d9-6813-421e-8263-ceb054511f3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1860abd2-6ed5-47b5-8c6f-c98c2242498f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75f481c6-11d1-4eb9-bca5-ea0c6b4765c5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If a student provides a different but plausible answer to the following scenarios, you can accept it, provide the expected answer is an alternate explanation.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75f481c6-11d1-4eb9-bca5-ea0c6b4765c5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1860abd2-6ed5-47b5-8c6f-c98c2242498f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1f68d75a-2cbc-4934-b032-c8143ca6a8d7",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario. Call on one or two students to suggest an answer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal that a classification model might be used to predict the kind of shoes students like.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario. Call on one or two students to suggest an answer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal that a regression model might be used to predict students’ ability and adjust the questions’ difficulty.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario. Call on one or two students to suggest an answer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal that a regression model might be used to predict athletes’ future performance based on their past performance.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This scenario is the trickiest: if students are struggling, you can provide some scaffolding by highlighting that most teams collect statistics and data about their players and prompting students to explore what that data might be being used for."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1f68d75a-2cbc-4934-b032-c8143ca6a8d7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1860abd2-6ed5-47b5-8c6f-c98c2242498f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76df2227-260e-4156-ae71-7981abcc94f0",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76df2227-260e-4156-ae71-7981abcc94f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1860abd2-6ed5-47b5-8c6f-c98c2242498f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97ef01c6-8152-4a03-b424-d814f1ac9413",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Since ML is being used to make decisions all around us, we need ways to ensure that it’s making the “right” decision or prediction and not being unfair, unsafe, or harmful in some other way.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97ef01c6-8152-4a03-b424-d814f1ac9413",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1860abd2-6ed5-47b5-8c6f-c98c2242498f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1a7fcde-2e18-4a41-904d-73279780d1fb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students build skills to evaluate the output of ML models based on the context in which they are used. The activities are designed to encourage students to consider principles such as safety and fairness in preparation for defining the terms more formally. Each activity in the guide focuses on a different way in which an ML model can have undesirable outputs. The first explores how important it is to identify useful categories when setting up a classification task. The second explores the idea that minimizing error in a regression model doesn’t always make the model more useful. The third pushes students to think about the kinds of data that are used in a model when making decisions. And the final activity asks students to more broadly articulate how they would evaluate a model if they knew it was being used to make decisions about them.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Prompt students to pair up and give each pair a copy of the Evaluating Models Ethically Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the instructions to explain how the activity will unfold.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For each activity, encourage students to take their time exploring the two models. For students that are struggling with the comparison questions, prompt them to first identify the differences between the models.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What are some questions you can ask when trying to determine if a machine is making the “right” decision?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Debrief students’ responses to the free-response question in Level 5. Spend some time on this slide, encouraging students to share what they wrote and prompting them to respond to see connections between each other. If students rely on evaluation metrics like minimizing error, push them to reflect on what the ML model is being used for and how they might evaluate if it’s outputs are fair or safe within that context. Strong responses might highlight that a machine is making the “right” decision if it’s not minimizing harm instead of error, or say that the “right” decision depends on the context.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Evaluating Models Ethically",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If any of the questions touch on ideas of fairness and safety, highlight those responses before moving onto the next slide. You can say something like “We’re going to explore two principles that help us evaluate the output of models to ensure that they aren’t harmful”."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1a7fcde-2e18-4a41-904d-73279780d1fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "75d40de3-d663-43bd-9757-55305267c758"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4518fe78-74ba-45a5-88f7-336777f73099",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *fairness*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *safety*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s Career Journeys connection: a safety engineer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4518fe78-74ba-45a5-88f7-336777f73099",
        "lesson_activity.key": "75d40de3-d663-43bd-9757-55305267c758"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22632734-e148-43ef-8902-ccbb29589111",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What principles other than fairness and safety are important to you when evaluating the output of an ML model?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** To push students to reflect on their own values and come up with metrics that they care about when evaluating ML models. If students are struggling, you may reframe the question as “Aside from being unfair or unsafe, are there other ways in which models can be harmful?”. You may also share examples such as:\n1. A music recommendation model that only recommends the kind of music that you listen to might mean you don’t discover new songs, which might be a problem if you value variety.\n1. Social media personalization algorithms can inadvertently promote polarization, if you value equality or shared perspectives this is a problem.\n1. Algorithms can inadvertently expose personal information about individuals in the training data, if you value privacy this is a problem.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22632734-e148-43ef-8902-ccbb29589111",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c22c566d-d099-4d6a-bd6f-9729f71b9bd0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "460b435b-7538-4cf6-bae1-a67a365481ce",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce the scenario that students will be working with for the lesson. Students start thinking about how to choose which variables to feed into a regression model.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the snack selection scenario.",
        "duration": "3",
        "name": "Picking Snacks"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "460b435b-7538-4cf6-bae1-a67a365481ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52e26812-fb4d-4874-b1dd-e7913f7cbff5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Our school is trying to decide which snacks to stock the school vending machine with.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52e26812-fb4d-4874-b1dd-e7913f7cbff5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e8da12a-ee21-406d-82e6-8ddda24c01d1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the ten possible snacks for the vending machine."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e8da12a-ee21-406d-82e6-8ddda24c01d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46e33eb0-0b53-4e63-bda4-db1073fc0464",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Practice interpreting a single-variable regression model together, introduce the vocabulary for the lesson.",
        "duration": "7",
        "name": "Exploring a Regression Model Together"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46e33eb0-0b53-4e63-bda4-db1073fc0464",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "49282906-ac21-4500-853c-1466ee10cfd3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this case, the school decided to use machine learning to help predict snacks students will like best. Here’s the model the school used in their first attempt. It predicts a rating score for a snack based on its price.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "49282906-ac21-4500-853c-1466ee10cfd3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c13a2aa9-1584-412a-a4dc-e070248223c9",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Based on this model, if the price of a snack is high, will the rating be high or low?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c13a2aa9-1584-412a-a4dc-e070248223c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92330353-106f-4fc0-8d13-24873fa67a81",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal that, if the price of a snack is high, its rating will be high."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92330353-106f-4fc0-8d13-24873fa67a81",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dfac7a18-052c-4feb-9c0f-f973e3dd81ee",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If we were to pick the highest rated snacks based on this model, we’d pick the most expensive ones.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are struggling, trace the regression line from left to right and point out that as the price goes up, the rating also goes up. You could also gesture to demonstrate what the opposite relationship might have looked like."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dfac7a18-052c-4feb-9c0f-f973e3dd81ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64852a7c-badf-47a3-9c9b-ecfd0c703ad6",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *interpretability*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64852a7c-badf-47a3-9c9b-ecfd0c703ad6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09c18d3c-503c-43e5-aa2a-c30a763763c2",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Looking at the model, we could see relatively easily that it predicted that snacks that cost more money will be more popular. That’s why it picked the most expensive snacks as the ones to stock in the vending machine. Some types of models are harder to interpret than others, and this makes it more challenging to figure out who to hold accountable for the model’s decisions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09c18d3c-503c-43e5-aa2a-c30a763763c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36c9aa8e-7762-42c7-b5ad-47e7600fe97e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *accountability.*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *After the school used this model, parents began to complain that the vending machine only had the most expensive snacks. Who should be held accountable for the vending machine only stocking expensive snacks?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Practice applying the principle of accountability. Possible strong answers include:\n1. We should hold the person who built the model accountable because they could tell that the model that was predicting that expensive snacks would be popular.\n1. We should hold the person who decided to use the model accountable because they could tell that the model was predicting that expensive snacks will be popular.\n\nAs students explore options for accountability, encourage students to suggest multiple plausible answers instead of pushing for agreement on just one. As the discussion proceeds, if it feels appropriate, you can ask whether the model’s interpretability makes a difference for who should be held accountable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36c9aa8e-7762-42c7-b5ad-47e7600fe97e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7cab16a-f8b6-4be5-aff0-13d80fae3928"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "faa75996-052c-4904-81a8-f7b115e7a693",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build regression models and reflect on how their decisions influence the predictions made by models. Students build familiarity with concepts like interpretability and accountability through practice and reflection.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Predicting Snack Ratings"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "faa75996-052c-4904-81a8-f7b115e7a693",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56fb5e8-d02f-4451-91d4-9702dab98c60"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76ddbe59-04e5-4904-a01d-bcdf99b566f6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The school has asked you to propose an alternative model to pick the snacks for the vending machine. You should be accountable to parents and students for the decisions you make, so be prepared to justify your decisions!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76ddbe59-04e5-4904-a01d-bcdf99b566f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56fb5e8-d02f-4451-91d4-9702dab98c60"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "65d2c5eb-5f7e-4653-b73d-0f344cff520a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s what the training data for your model looks like. For each snack in the training data, we know the shelf life, price, amount of protein, calories, number of allergens, and spice level (out of ten) as well as a popularity rating (also out of ten).",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "65d2c5eb-5f7e-4653-b73d-0f344cff520a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56fb5e8-d02f-4451-91d4-9702dab98c60"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93be9cd9-1ea8-4d13-8367-cb5d496b1817",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "For the ten snacks in our test data, we have all of the same information except for the popularity rating. The regression model you build will predict the rating. Then, we can pick the 5 snacks with the highest predicted rating for our vending machine.  When you’re building your model, you will pick which characteristic to use to predict the rating.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Invite students to ask questions about the scenario."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93be9cd9-1ea8-4d13-8367-cb5d496b1817",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56fb5e8-d02f-4451-91d4-9702dab98c60"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca413f85-8f32-4ea1-b683-f940aba12ae1",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Predicting Snack Ratings"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca413f85-8f32-4ea1-b683-f940aba12ae1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56fb5e8-d02f-4451-91d4-9702dab98c60"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0911c71e-de3e-4423-a536-ee6d55e393c6",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Levels 1–2.\n- **Level 1:** Students build a single-variable regression model to predict snack ratings and explore different characteristics.\n- **Level 2:** Students practice interpreting the model and justifying their decisions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, look for students who:\n- Get stuck passing valid characteristic names to the helper functions. Help them ensure that they are using the copying the characteristic names exactly, otherwise the helper functions won’t recognize the characteristics as valid.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What was your “best” model and how did you choose it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students explore how different people might have made different decisions as they were picking the “best” model. Students are likely to provide different explanations for their choices. Take at least 3 or 4 different responses and encourage students that made different choices to debate their ideas with each other. Strong answers might include:\n1. I chose a model that was a good fit for the training data. I picked it because I think it will be the most accurate.\n1. I chose the model that preferred snacks with the most calories. I picked that because I think it’s important that we get food that gives us a lot of energy.\n1. I chose the model that preferred snacks with the fewest allergens. I picked it because I think we should make sure that we don’t have lots of allergens in our snacks for safety.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As students explain their decisions and debate their ideas, point out when their arguments reflect their own values and aren’t purely mathematical (i.e. “I picked allergens because I thought it would be important”)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0911c71e-de3e-4423-a536-ee6d55e393c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56fb5e8-d02f-4451-91d4-9702dab98c60"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df8cdd37-d212-4ed8-8c59-43635a75126e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If someone used the vending machine and ate a snack that harmed them in some way, could we hold you accountable? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Practice articulating accountability and responsibility for the decisions made by a model. The goal of this discussion is to illustrate that accountability can fall on any of the people involved (those building the model, those using the model) but not on the model itself. If students suggest that the model itself should be held accountable, ask what that might look like in practice. Strong responses to the discussion question include arguing that the person creating the model should be held accountable (“I’m accountable because I chose the characteristic the model used to pick snacks”) or that those using the model should be accountable (“I explained how my model makes decisions, and it’s up to the school to decide whether or not to use it”).",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Direct the discussion so that it focuses on accountability, rather than harm. Allergen count is the feature of the data set students are most likely to mention. Be direct that students do not need to share their personal medical information to make a point in this discussion."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are only exploring one possible answer, present them other possibilities and ask for their thoughts and reactions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df8cdd37-d212-4ed8-8c59-43635a75126e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56fb5e8-d02f-4451-91d4-9702dab98c60"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e060b3c9-11e2-4063-9dbd-9f95ddbaef93",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve explored models that predict the highest rated snacks, based on different features such as price, shelf life, and allergen count. All of the models we’ve explored have one thing in common: they pick the most highly rated snacks to put in the vending machine.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e060b3c9-11e2-4063-9dbd-9f95ddbaef93",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56fb5e8-d02f-4451-91d4-9702dab98c60"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13d0ae54-f7e4-4700-b40d-aabf7c51d004",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Should the school pick snacks based on their predicted ratings? Can you think of other things that we could predict about the snacks instead of their ratings that you think would result in better decisions?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Explore whether the model is optimizing for the “right” output given the context. Strong answers provide alternatives or justifications that defend the existing model. Strong responses will either defend the current approach with justification (“I think that predicting ratings is the best because the snacks that the school provides should be ones that the students will like the most, even if some of them have allergens.”)  or suggest reasoning for a new approach (“I think it would be better to predict something like a health score, then instead of the most highly rated snacks, the vending machine would have the healthiest snacks”). Weaker responses will take a stance without offering justification: encourage these students to talk through their thinking and explain their reasoning."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13d0ae54-f7e4-4700-b40d-aabf7c51d004",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b56fb5e8-d02f-4451-91d4-9702dab98c60"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "868dada4-55d1-4904-a46e-7944eae8f114",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Tease the existence of models with varying levels of  interpretability to explore how issues of accountability get more nuanced as models get more complex.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the slide.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Varying Interpretability"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "868dada4-55d1-4904-a46e-7944eae8f114",
        "lesson_activity.key": "50b9f70d-8180-4bc1-98dd-edae7d0a7ce4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "895e8457-0404-404d-abd9-72764a4827b7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Other types of machine learning models can be harder to interpret than the ones we saw today.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "895e8457-0404-404d-abd9-72764a4827b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "50b9f70d-8180-4bc1-98dd-edae7d0a7ce4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e505df03-951d-494b-8c5e-5564a431de90",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here are some examples. On the left, we have a regression model that uses two variables to make a prediction. It’s not quite as simple to explain as the single-variable version. And on the right is one visualization of a Large Language Model (the kind of model that AI chatbots use). As you can see, it’s hugely complex and hard to interpret. It’s an ongoing area of research to try to understand them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e505df03-951d-494b-8c5e-5564a431de90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "50b9f70d-8180-4bc1-98dd-edae7d0a7ce4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ba46d42a-d6bb-441f-8108-9cd0d039930e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the reflection question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ba46d42a-d6bb-441f-8108-9cd0d039930e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "50b9f70d-8180-4bc1-98dd-edae7d0a7ce4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e41eada1-dda4-450d-bd33-ea50ce04deea",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students start thinking about features of photos that could be used for classification.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How can you tell apart a picture of a river from a picture of the ocean?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students consider how details of an image might be used to identify the image. Strong responses will mention specific characteristics of an image that could be used to identify it (for example, the presence of waves, or the presence of land on both sides of the image). Weaker responses include answers that are particularly subject to interpretation (for example, whether the body of water is “big” or “small”) or that are circular in reasoning (for example, whether the water is “ocean water”). If students struggle to provide specific answers, prompt them with follow-up questions like “What do you notice in the river image that makes it different from the ocean image?” Answers do not need to perfectly distinguish between all possible images of rivers and oceans: answers that provide a useful rule can still be strong even if they don’t work in all cases.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e41eada1-dda4-450d-bd33-ea50ce04deea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14cd9649-2d61-4e62-bfa1-09c1c38782f8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Over the next few days, you’ll have a chance to build a system that can perform classification tasks like this.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14cd9649-2d61-4e62-bfa1-09c1c38782f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "850903ef-39d3-4102-b718-c858303b3d7e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "850903ef-39d3-4102-b718-c858303b3d7e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3231f6ca-24e8-4941-8642-63be9e4ee799",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students start planning their image classifier by deciding what it will classify and brainstorming what kinds of features it might look for.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Category Brainstorming"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3231f6ca-24e8-4941-8642-63be9e4ee799",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "051c31b1-a394-494f-b218-70259fcaf4bc",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Image classifiers can tell apart different categories of images. For example, a classifier might take an image as input and predict whether the image is of a river or of an ocean.\n\nTo help our AI classify images, we can label those images with features.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "051c31b1-a394-494f-b218-70259fcaf4bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce7c1584-079a-4a5d-91ed-45e3abe65cdd",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the example feature for classifying rivers and oceans: Are there white waves in the water?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Advance to the next animation to show each image labeled with the feature.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that an AI might learn that if there are white waves in the water, the image is probably an ocean and not a river. Also point out that the feature isn’t perfect: for example, sometimes a river might have white waves too."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce7c1584-079a-4a5d-91ed-45e3abe65cdd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4406e39b-31c9-4f0a-a601-5628703fd017",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We can make our models better by using multiple features.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4406e39b-31c9-4f0a-a601-5628703fd017",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b2cdc84d-46fd-4f55-b932-d9c45f6ebaa5",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce a second example of a feature: Are there trees next to the edge of the water?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Advance to the next animation to show each image labeled with the feature.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b2cdc84d-46fd-4f55-b932-d9c45f6ebaa5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52614081-aea9-4791-9ad0-4d3be1f16d66",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, it’s your turn to think about how you would build an image classifier. You can decide what two categories of images you want your classifier to tell apart.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52614081-aea9-4791-9ad0-4d3be1f16d66",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "69c680b5-9d44-4f0e-a36d-270d128dc5f1",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students examples of image categories to spark their thinking about possible image classifiers they can build.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This project is open-ended and lets students choose what they want to classify. If students have access to a search engine where they can look up images, they can pick any two image categories to classify. If students do not have access to an image search tool, then you should ask them to pick one of the four examples presented on the slide instead: the lesson will provide pre-prepared datasets for each of the four given examples.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "69c680b5-9d44-4f0e-a36d-270d128dc5f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "522757b2-f868-4a19-a3c0-dd3a18786783",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Hand out the Image Classifier Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Step 1 and Step 2 of their Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through Step 1 and Step 2 of their Project Planning Guide, help guide students through the brainstorming process:\n- If students struggle to come up with ideas in Step 1, prompt them to think about activities they enjoy, objects they like at home or at school, or foods they like.\n- The difficulty of the project can vary depending on how similar or different the image categories are. For students that are interested in more of a challenge, you might encourage them to think about images that might be harder to tell apart. For example: roses and peonies might be harder to tell apart than basketballs and soccer balls.\n- In Step 2, encourage students to use specific questions. If students struggle to come up with their list, prompt them with questions like: “How would you explain to someone younger than you how to tell apart these two categories?”\n- If students are concerned that their questions might not perfectly tell apart images, remind them that this is okay and to be expected. Features in a machine learning model don’t need to be perfect on their own: combining multiple features together can help to make a model more accurate.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students have the ability to search the internet for images, they can come up with their own image categories in Step 1. If students won’t have the ability, direct them to choose from the four examples provided as part of the lesson instead.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "522757b2-f868-4a19-a3c0-dd3a18786783",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72264304-2213-405c-9866-b90cc73305df",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the project requirements and project rubric.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72264304-2213-405c-9866-b90cc73305df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9502ce24-a6aa-4302-ad1f-0b47ad012a60",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Data Collection",
        "progression_name": "Collecting Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9502ce24-a6aa-4302-ad1f-0b47ad012a60",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d02e0a05-124e-4a47-a941-c771becdf70c",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Students talk with an AI chatbot to refine their question list and brainstorm more useful features for their image classification task.\n- Level 2: Students collect image data and revise their question list based on the images they see. Students can use a pre-prepared dataset or collect their own dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look and listen for students who aren’t sure how to determine if a question will be a good feature or not. Help them think through whether their question meets characteristics of good features: a good feature should be clear to identify (e.g. “there are white waves” is better than “there are very big waves”) and useful for determining what category an image belongs to (e.g. “the water is blue” likely doesn’t help to tell apart rivers and oceans)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d02e0a05-124e-4a47-a941-c771becdf70c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e218518a-cf6a-45d6-a13b-a3f038d1d8c3",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to complete the Day 1 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e218518a-cf6a-45d6-a13b-a3f038d1d8c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2cbb9839-03ce-4d18-a72e-f85e36a19fcf"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3570c385-94e9-4f6f-9591-970cc116bde2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students look at a labeled dataset and make a prediction with it. Students familiarize themselves with the structure of the labeled image data they will work with in their image classifier project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Do you think Image 5 is a picture of an ocean or a river? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to discuss the data in pairs with a neighbor first, then bring the class together for a full discussion.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students make a prediction about what category an image falls into by inferring from labeled data. A strong answer makes a prediction and gives reasoning that compares it to other known data points (for example, “I think the image is of the ocean because it’s most similar to image 1” or “I think the image is of the ocean because it has no trees by the water and doesn’t have land on both sides”). Weaker responses will only identify a category without reasoning, or have reasoning that focuses only on one feature without considering the dataset as a whole. If students struggle to interpret the dataset, walk through how to interpret each row: each row represents an image, and each yes/no value represents a feature of that image. Help students see that images with similar features might belong to similar categories.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3570c385-94e9-4f6f-9591-970cc116bde2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b776f13-fb4c-492e-be52-6feaeb8a52f3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Computers can look at a collection of features and make predictions with it too. Today, we’ll use an approach like this to build our image classifiers.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b776f13-fb4c-492e-be52-6feaeb8a52f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e4f646b-308f-4652-8107-6bc65859a141",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students create a labeled data table that will later be converted to code to build a classification model. Students get practice labeling data with categories and features.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to pull out their Project Planning Guide from Day 1. Students will also need to access the images in their dataset from Day 1.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Labeling Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e4f646b-308f-4652-8107-6bc65859a141",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6f824d9-aeed-4158-8c6e-3025649e2ce1",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To help our AI classify images, we’re first going to convert those images into a list of features. Last time, you decided on some questions to ask about each image. Today, we’ll turn those images and questions into a table of features that AI can learn from.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6f824d9-aeed-4158-8c6e-3025649e2ce1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20329b05-c100-4c95-ad88-036d04279b2e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Step 3 of their Project Planning Guide. Students will use the images they collected on Day 1 and turn them into a table of labeled features.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through Step 3, help them fill in their data table:\n- In the header row, students should fill in the names of Feature 1, Feature 2, and Feature 3. If students brainstormed more than three questions, encourage them to pick the three they think will be most useful for telling apart their two categories. Students don’t need to write the entire question in the header cells: just a few words as a reminder to them is enough.\n- For each image in their dataset, students should write the name of the category the image belongs to (for example: “River” or “Ocean”). Students should also write a “Yes” or “No” for each of the three feature cells, depending on whether or not the image has the feature for that column. For students who get stuck here, guide them through the process of looking at each picture and checking for the presence of each of the three features one at a time.\n- Students should label most of their images as training data and some of their images as test data. Help students ensure that their training and test datasets include images from both categories.\n- Students are only required to have 12 total images in their dataset. If they have more, they can use the blank rows at the bottom of the table to add additional images."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20329b05-c100-4c95-ad88-036d04279b2e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a844d6b-df6b-41e7-9878-b9c44577e000",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students build a nearest-neighbor classifier in Python to classify their labeled data.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Writing a Classifier",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Now, let’s think about how we could take your data and put it into a Python program.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a844d6b-df6b-41e7-9878-b9c44577e000",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f881f71-f975-43e8-9905-bde76d00ed64",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example code that defines a list of training images. Each training image is represented by a dictionary. Each dictionary has a category plus `True` or `False` values that represent the yes/no answers to each of the three features.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f881f71-f975-43e8-9905-bde76d00ed64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bad364dc-f396-4e25-b84a-ecdecf80f3bf",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In your Python program, you’ll pick different names for the categories and different names for each of the features. You’ll also have multiple dictionaries: one for each image in your training set.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The dictionary in this example spans two lines so that it fits visually, but dictionaries can also be written all on a single line or with each key on a new line. If students are confused by this, you can explain that Python allows line breaks inside of dictionaries. Python relies on the closing curly brace to know when the dictionary ends.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bad364dc-f396-4e25-b84a-ecdecf80f3bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "328fd890-2b3e-46cc-a164-febf6b9a6ac5",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’re going to use a nearest-neighbor algorithm to classify these images. To do that, we need some Python logic that takes two images and tells us how similar or different they are based on their features.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "328fd890-2b3e-46cc-a164-febf6b9a6ac5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89a0b73f-a7e2-4989-be7e-39b8eb390dc8",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to an example distance function. The purpose of the function is to take two images and calculate their distance: a bigger distance means the images are very different, a smaller distance means the images are similar.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain to students that the square bracket syntax is used to access a value inside a dictionary. For example, `image1[\"white_waves\"]` takes the `image1` dictionary and accesses the value for the `white_waves` key: in this case, either `True` or `False`.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does this distance function work?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students walk through the logic of the code and think through how it achieves its goal of estimating how similar or different two images are. Strong answers will identify the role of the variable and the conditional statements. The conditional statements check if the two images have the same feature values; if the images have different feature values, then the images are more different, so the distance increases. If students struggle to reason about the code, encourage them to focus on an individual piece of conditional logic first (prompt them with questions like “When will the condition on Line 3 be true?”). Once students have an answer (for example, “It’s true when one image has white waves but the other image doesn’t”), guide them to see how the result of that check affects the distance between the two images."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89a0b73f-a7e2-4989-be7e-39b8eb390dc8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e378fd4a-478d-4ab5-b853-aa1a79c262fa",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ll write a distance function like this for your own image features, so that your classifier can figure out how similar or different two images are.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students might point out that this distance function only compares two different features of the images. For students’ own code, they should write distance functions that compare all of the features in their images.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e378fd4a-478d-4ab5-b853-aa1a79c262fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef13fcde-e425-46bc-a7e0-2f0c74b29ec3",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Building a Classifier"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef13fcde-e425-46bc-a7e0-2f0c74b29ec3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "182c7f19-fc93-43a2-85a3-e260598b83f5",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Levels 3-6 to begin work on their classifier project. They will continue work on these levels the following day for any levels they don’t complete today.\n- Level 3: Students convert their training data into a list of Python dictionaries for use in a classification algorithm.\n- Level 4: Students implement a distance function for comparing two dictionaries of features.\n- Level 5: Students test their classifier by using their test data.\n- Level 6: Students complete reflection questions about their image classifier.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students begin work on implementing their classifier, keep an eye out for the following:\n- Some students might struggle with converting their data into dictionaries. Remind students that dictionaries are a way to group related pieces of information and help students work through the syntax of creating a dictionary and specifying its keys and values. You can also point students to the Coding Tip in Level 3 to help them construct their dictionaries.\n- Some students might be unsure what to name their features. The feature names — which will be used as the dictionary keys — can be any text, but should be descriptive of what the feature represents. Help students brainstorm possible names for their features and work with students to ensure that those names are used consistently: if different parts of the code use different feature names, the logic will likely not work.\n- Students might need help with the syntax for constructing a list of dictionaries. Remind students that items in a list need commas to separate each item, so a list of dictionaries will also need a comma after each dictionary.\n- Students might also struggle with the logic for designing their distance function. Help these students think through the definition of distance in this case: distance is a measure of how many features are different. Help students write conditional statements that check for different features and encourage them to use a variable to keep track of how many features are different.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Let students know that it’s okay if they don’t get through all of the levels today: most students should finish Level 3 and at least get started on Level 4. On Day 3 of the project, students will continue working through any remaining levels.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "182c7f19-fc93-43a2-85a3-e260598b83f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "49c1c06f-91cc-4658-88b9-9327d8365bea",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to complete the Day 2 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "49c1c06f-91cc-4658-88b9-9327d8365bea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "96189389-7199-4825-a769-1ff48363eae2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6af24a33-de98-4c7f-8c52-281da247d8ff",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we’re going to keep working on building our image classifiers. Let’s first review some of the syntax you’ll see in this project.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6af24a33-de98-4c7f-8c52-281da247d8ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68822f9-52a3-4f86-ba0d-dd4d07001ce7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d7c7b1a-1b7d-418b-b000-2386b12e7447",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What will be the output if `image1` and `image2` are the same?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice reading Python code with dictionaries and make a prediction for how a distance function might behave if the inputs are the same. The goal is both to help students feel more comfortable with dictionary syntax and also to help them reason about using a numeric distance to compare sets of features. Strong answers will identify that the distance is 0 and give a reason why (for example: “all the features will be the same, so the distance variable will never increase”). Weaker answers might not make the connection between the output and the code (students might guess a nonzero distance, or guess a zero distance but not understand how the code leads to that result). Guide students to think through whether each condition will be `True` or `False` if both images have the same values for all features."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d7c7b1a-1b7d-418b-b000-2386b12e7447",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68822f9-52a3-4f86-ba0d-dd4d07001ce7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbee671b-1c8d-4901-8fe1-40ca80b6a8c0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When the two inputs are exactly the same, the distance is 0. When two images are similar but not exactly the same, the distance might not be 0, but it’ll probably be small. Your AI classifier will use that fact to help it make predictions.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbee671b-1c8d-4901-8fe1-40ca80b6a8c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68822f9-52a3-4f86-ba0d-dd4d07001ce7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50e689a4-dbb7-4e51-9b33-86e349ce5b90",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to pull out their Project Planning Guide from Days 1 and 2.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13 to continue work on Levels 3-6.\n- Level 3: Students convert their training data into a list of Python dictionaries for use in a classification algorithm.\n- Level 4: Students implement a distance function for comparing two dictionaries of features.\n- Level 5: Students test their classifier by using their test data.\n- Level 6: Students complete reflection questions about their image classifier.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students continue work on implementing their classifier, keep an eye out for the following:\n- Some students might struggle with converting their data into dictionaries. Remind students that dictionaries are a way to group related pieces of information and help students work through the syntax of creating a dictionary and specifying its keys and values. You can also point students to the Coding Tip in Level 3 to help them construct their dictionaries.\n- Some students might be unsure what to name their features. The feature names — which will be used as the dictionary keys — can be any text, but should be descriptive of what the feature represents. Help students brainstorm possible names for their features and work with students to ensure that those names are used consistently: if different parts of the code use different feature names, the logic will likely not work.\n- Students might need help with the syntax for constructing a list of dictionaries. Remind students that items in a list need commas to separate each item, so a list of dictionaries will also need a comma after each dictionary.\n- Students might also struggle with the logic for designing their distance function. Help these students think through the definition of distance in this case: distance is a measure of how many features are different. Help students write conditional statements that check for different features and encourage them to use a variable to keep track of how many features are different.\n",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Build and Evaluate a Classifier",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students finish early, encourage them to improve their classifier. For example, students might add more data to the training set so that it can make more accurate predictions. Students might also change the features they use to describe each image, or increase the number of features to improve the classifier’s accuracy.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50e689a4-dbb7-4e51-9b33-86e349ce5b90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68822f9-52a3-4f86-ba0d-dd4d07001ce7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1caae676-5bd6-49d4-8ff3-f1325ac67e61",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1caae676-5bd6-49d4-8ff3-f1325ac67e61",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e68822f9-52a3-4f86-ba0d-dd4d07001ce7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09b9a590-f45f-4477-9486-8d332e0c0b74",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
